WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.910
- Will humans, you know, forever be able

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.260
to contribute something

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.660
that an AI just could not have contributed,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.493
right? - Yeah.

00:00:07.493 --> 00:00:10.170
- And anyone who was confident of that, you know,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.810
after everything that's happened in the last few years,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.610
I don't know where their confidence comes from.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.550
You know, what people confidently claimed

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.310
were the walls, you know, that AI is hitting

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.900
have just been smashed through, right?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.750
We're now living in the wreckage of all of these walls,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.880
you know, that were proclaimed.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.340
And so, you know, maybe in a few years,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.610
AI is better not only at finding the proofs,

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.440
but at explaining the proofs.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.293
Maybe it's even better at coming up with new questions.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.190
- Some are calling it the mathocalypse,

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.400
others referring to the mathacre.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.350
Regardless of terminology, the reference

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.500
is of course to the recent onslaught by AI

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.760
tackling problems at the forefront of mathematics research

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.690
that to this point have been beyond the capacities

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.760
of human mathematicians.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.100
And look, some of these problems

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.350
have been career defining challenges,

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:08.820
problems that researchers have been chipping away at,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.290
you know, paper by paper, year by year, result by result.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.990
And then, AI comes along and bulldozes the problem,

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:20.550
ostensibly wiping away the problem by providing the answer.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:25.200
So, is this the apocalypse for mathematics?

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.000
Well, there are many issues and angles here.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.290
Are we confident that the AI proofs are correct?

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.980
Can AI succeed on every sort of problem

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.160
or are there common qualities of those problems

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.020
in which it is able to make headway

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.480
and perhaps even qualities of those

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.540
in which it may hit a wall?

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.270
Should we be all doom and gloom here

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:56.270
or perhaps do we simply need to get over our need

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.790
for human brains to be the problem solvers

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.170
and simply be excited by a powerful new tool

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.700
for progress?

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.040
Will mathematics of the future

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.920
become more like other scientific disciplines

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.850
like archeology in which the human role

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.890
is to find buried gems,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.860
in this case, wonderful theorems, understand them,

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.090
interpret their meaning,

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.300
but not to generate the gems themselves?

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.180
And more generally still,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.410
AI first made profound inroads into coding,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.480
now into mathematics.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.060
So, how much time do other fields have

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.060
before their AI come to Jesus moment is at hand?

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.540
To discuss these issues, I am joined by Scott Aaronson,

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.450
who heads the Quantum Information Center

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.670
at the University of Texas Austin.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.900
You may know Scott through his blog, Shtetl-Optimized

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.197
or his general level book,

00:02:56.197 --> 00:02:58.209
"Quantum Computing since Democritus."

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:02.760
And of particular relevance to our conversation here today,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.170
Scott spent two years at OpenAI on theoretical foundations

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.000
of AI safety.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.603
So my conversation with Scott Aaronson.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.430
Scott.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.263
- Hey.

00:03:15.263 --> 00:03:16.096
- Great to see you.

00:03:16.096 --> 00:03:16.929
- Great to see you again.

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:18.240
- Thanks for joining us here.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.520
Look, I wanted to start with something

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.690
that just happened the other day,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.130
which my daughter called me.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.160
She's a sophomore and she's following,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.720
she's not a math person,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.280
but she's a history major,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.850
but she's following the news, everything that's going on.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.230
And she said, "Dad, I don't even know how to think

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.260
about what the future is going to be."

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.150
And I said to her, "Sophia,"

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.540
I said, "Yeah, that's right.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.460
This may be the most,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.010
or at least among the most consequential moments

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.140
in the history of the human species."

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.750
I don't think I was exaggerating there.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.180
I mean, where do you put this?

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.070
- That has been the hardest thing

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.620
for me personally when young people,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.570
whether they're my students,

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:06.141
my own kids who are a little younger than yours,

00:04:06.141 --> 00:04:09.510
13 and nine, but they're well aware of what's happening,

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:10.343
right? - Yeah.

00:04:10.343 --> 00:04:14.490
- And when they ask, "What are humans going to be for?"

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:17.913
You know, apparently not for proving theorems.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:24.770
You know for writing, probably not for writing code.

00:04:25.688 --> 00:04:30.300
You know, what do we provide, right, either economically,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.150
if we're not going to be needed for anything economically,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.530
well then, you know, how should we think

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.684
about, you know, the point of life,

00:04:36.684 --> 00:04:37.517
you know? - Yeah.

00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:39.360
I guess we'll be consumers

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.956
no matter, you know- - Yeah.

00:04:40.956 --> 00:04:43.830
I mean, look, I think some people,

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.160
you know, could just happily live just consuming, you know,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.677
and consuming entertainment, you know, eating good food.

00:04:50.677 --> 00:04:53.670
You know, it wouldn't be the worst outcome in the world.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.950
You know, it's certainly better than the Terminators

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.300
you know, milling us all down,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.830
right? - Yes.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:04:59.940
- But I think a lot of people,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.490
you know, maybe had a higher aspiration than that, right?

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:05.460
That they're going to contribute some intellectual

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.790
or artistic work that only they could have contributed.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.640
So, you know, will those of us who felt that way

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.230
just need to get over ourselves?

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.410
- Well, that's I think is a good way of putting it.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.120
And I'd love to work our way back

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.210
to actually exactly that point because I do think

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.000
that part of it may be that we just need

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.990
to get over the certain kind of human exceptionalism

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.720
that once kind of defined us,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.370
at least for many as a species.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.730
But before we get there, I just wanna walk through

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:36.563
some of the developments.

00:05:36.563 --> 00:05:37.920
Look, many people are following this,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.700
but I think it's good to hear it from folks like you and me

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.650
who are sort of on the inside of this.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.420
Not in the sense of building the AI,

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.760
although you are far more on the inside than I,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.870
but in terms of math and physics soon

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.920
being disciplines where this is not a theoretical issue

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.157
of the impact, it's happening right now,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:05:59.220
you know? - Absolutely.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.300
- So if we go back, look, there was a time

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.720
in the distant past, by which I mean like a year or two ago,

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.720
when you know AI could do well on high school math,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.090
and then gold medal level

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.943
at the Olympiad-type questions and so on.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.810
At that point, people were still of a mindset of, well,

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:28.910
okay, it's a little bit of a parlor trick maybe and so on.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.760
But when we start getting to research level problems,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:33.720
that begins to shift.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.890
So, what was the first one that caught your attention?

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.560
- Well, okay, I mean, my attention was caught

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.190
since GPT-3 five years ago, right,

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:47.640
even though it was still struggling with arithmetic, right.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.420
You could basically say if scale has gotten us to this,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.877
then more scale is gonna get more and more.

00:06:53.877 --> 00:06:56.460
And so, I kind of saw the writing on the wall.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.700
And I had friends who were much earlier than that, right,

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.730
but I was then trying to warn people about it.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:06.570
And with each step, each thing that happens,

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:07.620
you're absolutely right.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.950
People just invent a reason why it doesn't really count,

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:11.783
right? - Right.

00:07:11.783 --> 00:07:14.040
- They say, okay, fine, of course it could do this.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.020
No one ever denied that it could do this.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.700
Of course, they themselves may have denied it.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:18.540
- Years ago

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:19.620
or six months, yeah. - Six months ago, right.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.380
But okay, but it's never gonna do that.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:24.540
And then six months later, it does that.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.420
And then, they invent a new excuse and so on.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.380
But I think for the math community at large,

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.720
you know, a major, major moment

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.083
came in the ancient time of May of this year.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:41.700
And that was when OpenAI announced the solution

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.843
to what was called the Erdos unit distance

00:07:43.843 --> 00:07:44.880
problem, yeah. - Unit distance problem, sure.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:45.713
- [Brian And Scott] Yeah.

00:07:45.713 --> 00:07:48.300
- Which was about, you know, if you have end points

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.030
in the Euclidean plane, how many pairs of them

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.631
can be distance one apart?

00:07:52.631 --> 00:07:57.570
Famous mathematician, Paul Erdos asked this in 1946.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:59.850
You know, he had a conjecture about the answer

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:03.870
that it's just maximized by putting them in a square grid.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:07.140
And then, what the AI found

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:09.180
is that that conjecture is wrong.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.000
There's a better way that gets more pairs

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.570
and it applied results

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:20.570
from a fairly obscure part of algebraic number theory right,

00:08:20.597 --> 00:08:22.050
to this problem.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:25.410
And so, you know, on the one hand, you could say,

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.080
well, it was just sort of using standard techniques,

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.960
just not techniques that any experts in this problem

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.220
would have known, right>+?

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.300
But on the other hand,

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.160
that's what a lot of human math is about.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.200
It's about finding connections.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.970
And there was no question

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.077
that if a human mathematician had done that,

00:08:41.077 --> 00:08:44.640
you know, they would have gotten tenure at any department.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.607
It would have been published in Annals of Mathematics.

00:08:47.607 --> 00:08:50.190
And so, there was a whole bunch of commentaries on it

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.740
by Timothy Gowers, Noga Alon,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.600
you know, famous mathematicians

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.977
just sort of trying to understand,

00:08:56.977 --> 00:08:59.490
you know, the impact of what had happened.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:02.250
And that was when this became impossible

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:06.930
to, I think, for the working researchers to ignore.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.790
And I remember when I heard about it,

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.340
I was in my office at UT Austin

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.537
and some students just came and they said,

00:09:13.537 --> 00:09:16.080
"Have you seen this news about unit distance?"

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.250
And I said, "You know what?"

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:20.130
Because I don't check Twitter every 15 minutes,

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:23.430
but I saw.

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:27.060
And the students, I have to say, were kind of despondent.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.540
They were immediately sort of extrapolating this

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.340
to what is our future.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:36.090
And half a year later,

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.400
I'm still trying to figure out

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.027
what was the right word- - And no doubt we will be

00:09:41.027 --> 00:09:41.860
for a while.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.710
- Right, And then, I would say what happened

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:46.140
is over the course of this summer,

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.440
you know, we saw more and more shoes drop,

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.390
more and more problems

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:55.920
that we thought were incredibly hard just fall to AI models.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:59.730
Sometimes, it was a combination for human and AI,

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.820
you know, what's called a centaur, okay?

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.700
Sometimes, it was just pure AI one shotting,

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.490
as in, you know, someone feeds the question

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:11.220
into GPT-6 or whatever.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.920
And then an hour later it spits out the solution.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:14.753
- Right. - Okay.

00:10:14.753 --> 00:10:17.700
So, that included

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.410
some of my favorite problems

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.060
in quantum computing theory.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:26.060
I saw, you know, one by one, started seeing them get solved.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.610
And by the end of the summer, I think every new paper,

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.280
we just expected that at the end of it,

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.277
there's this little AI usage statement, right?

00:10:35.277 --> 00:10:37.710
And this is where the authors disclose,

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:41.940
you know, which AIs did they use to do this research

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:43.560
and for which parts of it,

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:44.460
right? - Right.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.770
- And where they would assure the reader

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.070
that they take full responsibility for the results,

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:50.970
you know, right? - Right.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:54.756
- And so, yeah, that is now a new convention of academia

00:10:54.756 --> 00:10:57.810
that, you know, it wasn't really debated or discussed,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.940
but that's, you know, how we do it now.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.640
- But let me ask you one question.

00:11:02.499 --> 00:11:06.330
So on the unit distance problem, it was disproving

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.163
conjecture- - It was,

00:11:07.163 --> 00:11:08.730
yes, it was. - And Navier-Stokes,

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:11.460
which many people have read about "New York Times,"

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.860
and everything, those millennium math problem,

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.180
it was finding a counter

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:16.470
example. - That's right.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.720
- And so, you can begin to think

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.610
that maybe AI is really good at that kind of a problem.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:24.443
- [Scott Aaronson] Yeah.

00:11:24.443 --> 00:11:25.740
- Is that too limited?

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:30.740
- Well, I think it's true that AI has been especially good

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.400
at finding counter examples-

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:33.233
- On the Jacobian-

00:11:33.233 --> 00:11:34.940
- Yeah, that's right. That's right.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:39.060
And I think a good way to think about this

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:43.800
is just, you know, AI has been especially good

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:47.190
at any problem that requires trying thousands

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:50.700
of different weird constructions or weird examples

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.752
until it finds one that works.

00:11:52.752 --> 00:11:55.830
Because unlike us, it never gets tired.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:59.040
It never gets discouraged by the fact that experts

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.680
think that this construction doesn't exist,

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.190
you know, which from experience is huge for us,

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:06.023
right? - Of course.

00:12:06.023 --> 00:12:08.250
- But it just keeps trying and trying,

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.280
you know, until it finds some bizarre thing that works.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.140
And if that takes too long,

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.800
you can always spin up a thousand more agents,

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:19.590
you know, to try more examples in parallel, right?

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:23.040
So, the Navier-Stokes was like that.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:27.993
The Jacobian, as you said, the unit distance were like that.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.900
Even some of the conjectures that it's proved to be true.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.410
So my wife, Dana Moshkovitz,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.030
for the entire time I've known her,

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:40.620
she's been working on a problem

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.540
called the unique games conjecture.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:43.373
- What is that?

00:12:43.373 --> 00:12:45.630
- Okay, so it was posed by a guy

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:48.540
named Subhash Khot in 2001.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.400
And it basically says

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:55.400
that for a whole bunch of optimization problems,

00:12:56.190 --> 00:13:00.150
the best approximation that you can hope

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:04.320
to get to the optimal solution like in polynomial time

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.320
is the solution that you would get via a standard technique

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:14.130
called semi-definite programming unless P is equal to NP.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:15.330
- Okay, which is a big-

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:17.970
- Exactly, which is an even bigger question.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:21.810
So this is not directly addressing whether P is equal to NP,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.260
but it's saying assuming P is not equal to NP,

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.450
all these approximation problems

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.450
that we thought maybe are NP complete

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:31.920
really are NP complete.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:33.030
- Right. - Okay.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:37.200
And so, it was sort of the central conjecture

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:41.340
in this subfield called hardness of approximation.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:45.577
And so when I started dating my wife in 2009, she said,

00:13:45.577 --> 00:13:48.720
"I have to tell you, I'm working on this unique games."

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:50.187
And, you know, 16 years later-

00:13:50.187 --> 00:13:51.585
- And you were willing to

00:13:51.585 --> 00:13:52.590
accept that? - Yeah, that's right,

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:53.962
I was willing to accept that.

00:13:53.962 --> 00:13:56.010
I said, okay, okay, it's good-

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:57.270
- To get it out in the open now.

00:13:57.270 --> 00:14:00.363
- Yeah, exactly. Good to be open about it.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.950
And that was one of the crown jewels

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:09.420
of these 372 major open problems

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:13.290
that OpenAI published solutions to

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.680
in a GitHub repository two weeks ago.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.950
The event that I have called the mathocalypse,

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:21.960
although someone told me I should have called it

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.012
the mathacre.

00:14:23.012 --> 00:14:24.030
(Brian and Scott laughing)

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:24.863
- It works too.

00:14:24.863 --> 00:14:25.696
- Yeah, right.

00:14:25.696 --> 00:14:29.070
But sort of a crown jewel of this mathacre

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.100
was the solution to the unique games problem.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:32.933
And-

00:14:32.933 --> 00:14:35.070
- And where do we stand on that?

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.530
For instance, is there a Lean Certificate?

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.960
- For that one, there is a Lean Certificate.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:40.830
- And can you explain just

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:41.850
for folks, yeah? - Yeah, so

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:46.110
Lean is a way of verifying proofs

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.330
in a purely mechanical way, right?

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:51.090
So, it just writes the proof

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.340
in this sort of logical notation,

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.980
where, you know, you could just write a program to check

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:57.540
that yes, there is a valid proof.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.070
- So every inference step

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:00.360
is check. - Exactly.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.450
Now, there's still, you know, a couple things

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:05.790
that you could quibble about.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:07.620
You could say, was the statement

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:10.680
actually correctly encoded into Lean, right?

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.590
Does the thing that was proved actually match

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:15.660
the thing that we wanted to prove?

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:18.030
You know, there have been bugs in Lean

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:20.130
that have been discovered, you could worry about them.

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:23.760
You could worry about whether this logical system is sound,

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.430
which is actually not known.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:28.590
But modular, all of that, it gives us

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:30.810
a higher degree of assurance that yes,

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:34.140
there really is a proof here even if we don't understand it.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:36.960
- And the proof itself, I mean, I had this conversation

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:37.793
with Tristan

00:15:37.793 --> 00:15:39.000
Buckmaster- - Yes, that's right.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.860
- That when you actually look at the output,

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.443
and I looked at some of them to see what it looked like,

00:15:43.443 --> 00:15:45.480
it's very unreadable.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:46.313
- Yes.

00:15:46.313 --> 00:15:47.146
- Now, are...

00:15:47.146 --> 00:15:49.717
But I then looked at some of the papers on the GitHub.

00:15:49.717 --> 00:15:53.250
Some of them, again, they're outside my field per se,

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.020
but they seem much more readable.

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.270
So, that seems to be improving.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.070
- Yeah, so I think

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:03.330
like, you know, they are written in English, you know,

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:05.321
or in LaTeX, right?

00:16:05.321 --> 00:16:09.840
You know, they're at least locally comprehensible.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:13.800
But what my wife, Dana, has told me,

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.950
she has been spending all of her time these past few weeks

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.150
studying the OpenAI proof of the unique games conjecture.

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:21.090
- Is it like hundreds?

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.501
Is it 10 pages? - It's a...

00:16:23.501 --> 00:16:25.090
I don't remember how many, you know.

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:26.160
- It's hundreds, yeah. - It's some

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.910
dozens of pages, but she said it's as if it was written

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:31.890
by someone on psychedelics.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:34.440
You know, it's like the key claims are scattered

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:35.930
all over the place.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.640
The central ideas are hidden.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:41.010
She said there's basically no option

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:44.253
except to use AI to help understand the proof.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.960
- But AI is not yet at the stage, and I'm sure it will be.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.720
They can say, "Hey, here's the output.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.190
Here's the proof."

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.250
Now, you know how we humans like to write our papers

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.230
because you've read a gazillion of them.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.840
Can you translate from that

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:03.390
to a more systematic, you know...

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.448
- Yeah, like they're getting better at it.

00:17:06.448 --> 00:17:08.310
They're not there yet, right?

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.530
I feel like what they're missing maybe

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:13.680
is a really good model of the human reader, right?

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:16.800
What is an expert going to find obvious here?

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:17.970
What are they going

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:19.770
to need spelled out? - And they can't

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:20.850
extract that from...

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:22.530
- Yeah, I mean, okay, so to be honest,

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:24.090
like this is one of the hardest things

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:26.670
when we're teaching our grad students, right.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.560
They often do the exact same thing.

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.240
They write something that's brilliant

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.340
but, you know, barely comprehensible

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:34.860
or they spend pages, you know,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:36.990
spelling out something obvious

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:40.590
and then they skip over the essential point in one line.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:43.830
And this is one of the, you know, the final things

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.230
I would say that we teach our students.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:49.740
And I feel like it's similar with the AIs.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.770
Now, you could say as long as they're bad writers,

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:54.900
in a certain sense, that's great for us

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:57.240
because it means we still have something to do.

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.550
You know, in fact, we have more to do

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:01.656
than we can possibly handle right now

00:18:01.656 --> 00:18:04.740
in digesting all of these results.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:06.900
I don't know if digest is the right word.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.857
Some people said, "Well, what's the output of digestion?"

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:15.450
But, you know, interpret, interpret, understand.

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:18.600
Yeah, so I feel like even if nothing else had happened,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:21.600
which of course much more will happen,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.620
we could spend years just understanding the implications

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:28.710
of what OpenAI dumped onto GitHub a couple weeks ago.

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:30.030
- Now, no doubt.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:30.863
- Yeah.

00:18:30.863 --> 00:18:32.770
- 700 papers

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:36.960
is gonna next time be 10,000.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.890
- Yeah, well, I think what's likely is that the AI companies

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:44.100
you know, will often just not even bother to do this,

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:46.560
except maybe for the biggest problems

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.623
like the Riemann hypothesis or P versus NP.

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:53.760
The internal model that OpenAI used to solve

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.300
these 300 plus problems, I think is very similar

00:18:57.300 --> 00:18:59.580
to what any subscriber is gonna have, you know,

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.530
within the next half a year.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:02.790
And they're just gonna let

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:04.830
individual researchers feed in these

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:07.110
problems and solve them. - Just go and do it.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:09.600
- And we're gonna have to figure out what are the new norms

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:11.970
for this world for announcing these results.

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:13.260
- So I'd love to get there in a second,

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:16.980
but I want to, as far as we can, address

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:19.830
the kinds of questions that I'm always peppered with

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.590
whenever I have talked about this stuff,

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:25.530
which is what we began with,

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:28.290
AI really good at counter examples.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:31.980
Then people say, well, look, yeah, it's fine.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:33.690
I'll even guarantee it's impressive

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:36.156
that it can grab different ideas

00:19:36.156 --> 00:19:40.530
from the corpus of human past knowledge,

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:42.000
put it together in new ways.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.130
And whether you call that a rehash

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:46.440
or creativity as a matter of taste,

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.210
but get new results from that.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.380
But my question- - I mean, so many

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:50.400
of us do nothing more

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:51.510
than our whole career. - That's what

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:52.590
I agree, I agree.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.960
No, I'm fond of saying, and some people get mad at me,

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.690
that general relativity is a wonderful achievement,

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:58.740
but Einstein

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:01.350
took Riemannian geometry- - That's right.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:02.183
Right. - You know?

00:20:02.183 --> 00:20:03.330
- If Einstein had been in AI,

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:04.620
everyone would have said, "Well, look,

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:05.453
he just took-" - Of course.

00:20:05.453 --> 00:20:06.286
- "Riemannian.

00:20:06.286 --> 00:20:07.410
He took, you know, conquer.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:08.760
He just mashed things together."

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:10.470
- Exactly, exactly.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.113
So when you, from what you've seen,

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:17.913
how would you characterize the level of creativity?

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.960
- Yeah, so I think it has done things

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:24.360
that there is no question whatsoever

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.880
that if a human mathematician had done them,

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:27.947
then we would have called it creative,

00:20:27.947 --> 00:20:29.580
right? - Yeah.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:32.880
- And so even these weird counter examples that it finds,

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.160
right, you know, we would have called it creative

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:36.000
if a human did.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.497
And so with the unique games conjecture,

00:20:38.497 --> 00:20:42.150
you know, what Donna tells me is that the heart of it

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:46.380
is this weird new error correcting code that it constructs.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:49.230
And it may have tried a thousand things

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:50.970
before it found that code, you know.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:51.960
I'm not sure.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:53.490
But once it's found, you know,

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.117
if any human had come up with that,

00:20:55.117 --> 00:20:56.707
you know, we all would have said,

00:20:56.707 --> 00:20:59.070
"Oh, that's really clever. That's really creative."

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:00.090
- Yeah.

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:03.180
And you know, allied with that question

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:07.260
is results are great in any field.

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:08.093
- Yes.

00:21:08.093 --> 00:21:12.240
- But oftentimes, it's the steps along the way

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:15.480
that generate the new ideas, the new directions,

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:18.270
the new way of thinking about things.

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:21.690
Do you think that these AI solutions

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:24.570
are going to play that same role,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:28.650
that they will have sufficiently creative

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:32.460
or new ingredients that they will seed

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.620
the next collection of results

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:36.217
as opposed to just being,

00:21:36.217 --> 00:21:37.860
"Well, yeah, we bashed it together.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.800
We got the answer, but it isn't fruitful

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.210
because there's nothing we can mine

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.250
that we can go elsewhere with that result."

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.740
- I think that there's no question

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.320
that these results are going to advance

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.388
human mathematical understanding.

00:21:52.388 --> 00:21:56.490
On the other hand, right now, it is up to us as humans

00:21:56.490 --> 00:22:00.120
to sort of mine these things and to extract the insight

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.580
and the understanding from them, right?

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:06.000
So you know, one analogy that people use,

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:10.290
it's as if we now have this bizarre teleportation machine

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.210
you know, that can just take us immediately

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:14.010
to the top of all these mountains

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:15.658
that we were trying to climb.

00:22:15.658 --> 00:22:17.550
But what we actually cared about

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:19.290
was not standing on the top.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:21.960
It was knowing what is the path up,

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:22.793
right? - Yeah.

00:22:22.793 --> 00:22:25.170
- And now, with the help of the teleportation machine,

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.390
now we still have to go back and find the path.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:28.223
- Right.

00:22:28.223 --> 00:22:32.250
And do you think it likely that that role

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:34.290
will persist for a while?

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:36.420
Because again, it's almost the case

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:38.520
that almost any role that we put forward-

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.510
- Absolutely.

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:42.090
- We consider ourselves, well, just give AI

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:45.030
a little bit more time, a little bit more experience

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:46.710
on that kind of question.

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:50.790
Like if we say, "Hey, great solution to unique games,

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:52.680
great solution to unit distance,

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:53.670
have your stokes beautiful."

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:56.160
But now what we want you to really do

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:59.640
is lay out the steps in a way that mirrors

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:02.520
what human mathematicians have done throughout the ages

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:06.420
so that we can really understand the trajectory

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.930
and in that way be able to leverage the insights

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:12.097
for yet further discovery as opposed to saying,

00:23:12.097 --> 00:23:13.350
"We got the answer done.

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:15.360
We can forget about that problem."

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.700
Won't it get ever better?

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.760
- Yeah, so I think there are two questions here.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:25.470
One is will humans forever be able to contribute something

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:28.170
that an AI just could not have contributed?

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:31.890
And there, you know, anyone

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:33.510
who is confident of that,

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:35.810
you know, after everything that's happened

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:37.050
in the last few years,

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:39.397
I don't know where their confidence comes from, right?

00:23:39.397 --> 00:23:41.070
You know, like these things

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:42.787
keep getting better and better, right?

00:23:42.787 --> 00:23:46.650
You know, what people confidently claim were the walls,

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.980
you know, that AI is hitting have just been smashed through.

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:51.420
We're now living in the wreckage

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.990
of all of these walls, you know, that were proclaimed.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.500
And so, you know, maybe in a few years, AI is better,

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:00.720
not only at finding the proofs,

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.550
but at explaining the proofs.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:05.580
Maybe it's even better at coming up with new questions.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:06.570
I mean, for these things,

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:08.580
there's not an objective right answer

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:11.220
as to, you know, which question is better,

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.020
but maybe it will come up with questions

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:14.297
that you know, we find better,

00:24:14.297 --> 00:24:15.273
right? - Yeah.

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:18.765
- But then even if it's better

00:24:18.765 --> 00:24:21.540
at all the intellectual things

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.550
that human mathematicians do,

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.650
then, you know, there's still a second question,

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:28.470
which is do we want to keep doing math

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:30.330
because we think it is important

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:32.760
for humans to understand math,

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:33.990
you know? - Right.

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:37.500
- And you know, many people make the analogy to chess here.

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:41.520
Humans lost their supremacy at chess 30 years ago.

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:45.150
A lot of people predicted after Kasparov versus Deep Blue,

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:46.950
this is the end of chess.

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:48.720
No one will wanna play anymore.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:50.397
The opposite has happened, right?

00:24:50.397 --> 00:24:52.500
- But I wonder about that analogy.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:53.760
Obviously, I've heard it.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:55.380
I've thought about it a lot.

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:58.170
I wonder if it's a misleading analogy

00:24:58.170 --> 00:24:59.003
in the following sense.

00:24:59.003 --> 00:25:04.003
Chess begins as a intellectual combat sport, right?

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:05.820
You've got two players.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.610
And the goal is to win over your opponent,

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:12.000
whether your opponent is a human or an AI

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.790
or you know, someone well matched to you,

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:19.110
because often we set the different levels of achievements

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.900
so that people are reasonably well-matched.

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:24.750
Math is so different from that.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:27.090
Certainly, we do have competitions,

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:28.457
you know? - Yeah, that's right.

00:25:28.457 --> 00:25:29.790
- But in the end of the day,

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:32.820
most people do math not to win a competition.

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:37.110
They do it because they want to explore into the unknown.

00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:38.877
They want to take human knowledge

00:25:38.877 --> 00:25:40.770
to a place that's not been before.

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:42.930
And so since the goals are so radically different,

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.540
do you think chess is a good analogy

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:48.332
for where this might go?

00:25:48.332 --> 00:25:51.720
- I think it's good in some ways and not others, right?

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:54.330
If you talk to the highest level chess players,

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:56.220
often they are talking about, you know,

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:58.470
they would rather lose in an interesting game

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:00.030
than win in a boring game,

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:00.863
right? - Right.

00:26:00.863 --> 00:26:01.696
- You know, they're thinking

00:26:01.696 --> 00:26:03.780
about what are they understanding

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:04.833
from this game.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.400
But on the other hand, you know,

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.680
clearly it is a sport, right?

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:14.670
It is, I guess for some people, a recreation.

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:17.100
For others, a competition, right?

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:20.820
And I guess we always had this higher vision

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:23.550
of ourselves in math and in science

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.880
that we are discovering the truths of the universe.

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:31.320
And so, one question will be

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:33.570
do we still want to be doing that?

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:36.180
Another question is will society support us

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:40.800
to continue doing that in a world where AI does all of this?

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:45.187
So when I am giving students advice about this,

00:26:45.187 --> 00:26:47.820
you know, which I've been asked to do a lot

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:49.350
in these past few months,

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:53.100
you know, what I keep coming back to is that right now,

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:55.890
humans are absolutely needed.

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:59.070
You know, they're needed maybe more urgently

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:01.080
than at almost any time in the past.

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:06.080
And the reason, you know, or proof that we are still needed

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:08.160
is that we don't have AIs

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:12.630
that we know to be reliably aligned with our values,

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:13.463
right? - Right.

00:27:13.463 --> 00:27:15.360
- And as long as we don't, then that means

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.210
that we still have to be at the steering wheel

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:19.500
of the civilization,

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:20.333
right? - Right.

00:27:20.333 --> 00:27:23.250
- We still have to be looking at evaluating

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:26.640
the AI's outputs, trying to tell is it hallucinating,

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:28.500
is it lying to us, you know.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:31.620
And as long as we're needed, even just for that,

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:35.550
that means we need some of those humans to understand math,

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:38.790
to understand physics, to understand programming,

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:42.450
to understand all of the, you know, disciplines

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.220
that the humans have worked on,

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:47.040
even in those places where AI is now beating us,

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:48.120
right? - Right.

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:51.600
And so I would love to get to some of the issues,

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:53.700
especially on guardrails

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:55.410
that you've spent some time thinking about.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:59.550
But let me just, before we do that, in some sense,

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:03.450
if you have a platonic view of mathematics, which I don't,

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:05.673
but anyway, just to put it out there,

00:28:05.673 --> 00:28:07.770
I think you do more than I do

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:09.360
from previous conversation. - I'm a

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.460
Platonist about arithmetic.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:14.610
I'm a formalist about the other stuff for the most part.

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.820
- But if vaguely we say that mathematical truths

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:21.720
are kind of out there, they're already out there.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:24.990
In some sense, all AI has done

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:28.747
is pull the curtain back a little bit more quickly

00:28:28.747 --> 00:28:31.110
than humans could pull the curtain back.

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:32.370
The stuff was already out there

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:34.380
and it was just a matter of seeing it.

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:39.380
So to the person who says, "This is fantastic.

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:42.720
I wanted to pull the curtain back

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:45.600
and now, I've got this powerful tool for helping me too."

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:49.260
So, what are you all getting all, you know, upset about?

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:52.440
- Yeah, so there's been

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:56.490
a huge range of reactions within the math community.

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:58.560
And if you try to list the emotions

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:03.330
that they might be feeling, like exhilaration, excitement,

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:06.360
awe, you know, dread, anger,

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:08.190
I think you can find all of this,

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:10.940
you know, in different proportions in different people.

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:15.180
But you know, one contingent

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:18.000
that's been like very loud on social media,

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:21.720
right, has been the one that basically, you know,

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:22.987
says to the AI companies,

00:29:22.987 --> 00:29:24.960
"Get out of our playground.

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:27.030
Stop taking away our toys."

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:28.200
You know, there was this thing

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:31.110
called Association for Human Mathematics

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.917
that put out a statement that said,

00:29:32.917 --> 00:29:35.490
"The mathematical community did not ask

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:37.375
for these problems to be solved."

00:29:37.375 --> 00:29:40.830
And that was then widely mocked on social media.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:44.380
I think there was a lot of Schadenfreude, probably.

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:47.497
A lot of people saying,

00:29:47.497 --> 00:29:50.490
"Oh, you know, these poor mathematicians, they thought

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:54.870
that they were so far above the rest of humanity

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:58.320
and now they're being humbled by a mere robot,"

00:29:58.320 --> 00:29:59.266
and stuff right?

00:29:59.266 --> 00:30:01.110
You know, and people are kind of enjoying that.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:05.910
But I think, frankly, on the one hand,

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:07.920
you know, we have to figure out

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:10.800
how human understanding of math

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:14.250
goes forward as a culture, as a profession,

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:16.920
you know, assuming that that is something that we value,

00:30:16.920 --> 00:30:20.220
which I value it enormously myself.

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:23.220
We have to hold the AI company's feet

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:26.943
to the fire to behave well, to be ethical.

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:30.660
You know we have to worry enormously

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:34.110
about what effects all this is going to have,

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:36.840
not just on math, on our little world,

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:39.136
but on all of civilization, right?

00:30:39.136 --> 00:30:42.660
So yes, all of that, but at the same time,

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:46.620
you know, it is not our prerogative

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:48.480
to say who gets to work on our problems.

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:49.313
- Of course. - Right?

00:30:49.313 --> 00:30:50.730
Once a problem is out there,

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:52.830
anyone in the world can solve it,

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:55.140
even if they're a corporation that you don't like.

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:56.510
- I mean, do you see this in some sense

00:30:56.510 --> 00:30:58.440
as a great equalizer?

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:01.050
- It does have some of that, right?

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:04.380
Like there are a whole lot of amateurs who are excited

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:07.200
that now they can get involved in research math,

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:08.820
you know, with the help of AI.

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:12.540
I think that on balance, that may be a good thing, right?

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:16.290
But with the proviso that ultimately we do want

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:18.660
human understanding of what comes out.

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:20.100
- And that's a key question.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:23.250
An analogy that I've used that's imperfect

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:26.400
is Borges's "Library of Babel."

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:28.590
You know, You've got all these books,

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:32.580
410 pages made with whatever, 25 symbols

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:34.590
and whatever possible combination.

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:37.230
The vast majority of those books are uninteresting.

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:39.180
They're unreadable.

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:42.120
But there are these gems somewhere in the library

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:44.820
if only you could find them that would give you answers

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:48.300
to deep questions and the most wonderful novel

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:50.190
that was ever written is out there

00:31:50.190 --> 00:31:52.470
and it's just hard to find them.

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:56.043
Similarly, AI, you know, there are infinitely many theorems

00:31:56.043 --> 00:31:57.690
that can be proven.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:01.050
Many of them we humans wouldn't find interesting.

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:03.750
And so one role, and I hope this doesn't feel

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:06.540
like species-wide subcism or something,

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:10.200
but one role for us is we're the only ones

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:11.880
who know what we're interested in.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:13.950
We're the only arbiters of the things

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:17.760
that we want to spend our time understanding.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:19.470
That role can't be usurped.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:21.000
- Right, absolutely.

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:21.847
Right, so what makes

00:32:21.847 --> 00:32:24.780
"The Library of Babel," completely useless

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:26.640
is that if you just want to look up a book,

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:28.200
you have to write the book, right?

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:29.910
You have to specify, right?

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:31.470
So you could say that in some sense,

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:33.690
you know, we're already in "The Library of Babel,"

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:36.000
every time you open your word processor

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.220
and, you know, face a blank screen.

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:40.620
That's "The Library of Babel," right there.

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:43.350
But I mean, you know, this of course is different

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:45.480
because you just have to put in, you know,

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:47.160
the theorem that you want.

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:51.270
And then often enough out comes the proof of that theorem.

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:55.290
But you could say, look, all of these true theorems,

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:57.210
you know, for those of us who will use

00:32:57.210 --> 00:32:58.590
Platonus language, you know,

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.420
they were already there, you know,

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:03.320
or, you know, some people would say God already knew them.

00:33:04.650 --> 00:33:08.130
You know, the whole point was for us to know them, right?

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:10.800
And for us to know them, for most of us,

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:12.420
means to understand.

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:16.590
- So taking our side of it out of it,

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:20.580
is this not in some ways the golden age of mathematics?

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:23.033
- It is a golden age for finding solutions

00:33:23.033 --> 00:33:23.880
to- - And not

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:25.260
the one that we imagined.

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:27.300
- Yeah, no, but it's...

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:29.490
So the next few years, you know,

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:32.970
are going to be clearly a golden age

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:37.443
for solving so many of our longstanding open problems.

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:42.570
And look, to the extent that we actually cared

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:43.590
about these problems,

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:45.720
we actually wanted to know the answers,

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:47.640
which I think we did, you know,

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:51.180
then we ought to celebrate that, right?

00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:55.950
But, you know, I've compared it

00:33:55.950 --> 00:34:00.090
to like the most delicious buffet that you've ever tasted,

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:02.310
but the trouble is they just keep bringing

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:06.180
more and more trays of food, you know, every 10 seconds.

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:08.640
Eventually, you're just gonna get a stomach ache.

00:34:08.640 --> 00:34:10.800
You're just gonna keel over, right?

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.050
So like, you know, this delicious food,

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:16.500
it has to arrive at a pace where we can actually eat it,

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:17.490
right? - Right.

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:21.210
- And so, how can

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:23.610
our human understanding keep pace

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:24.983
with what is happening, right?

00:34:24.983 --> 00:34:27.420
I think that's the challenge that we face now.

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:29.160
- Now you've mentioned that problems

00:34:29.160 --> 00:34:32.460
that you have worked on, conjectures that you've made

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:33.930
as I understand it in fact.

00:34:33.930 --> 00:34:36.210
A problem that your wife has worked on

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:38.640
what now you said since 2004

00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:40.689
she was- - Since 2008,

00:34:40.689 --> 00:34:41.522
I think. - 2008

00:34:41.522 --> 00:34:42.355
So, it was like- - She worked on the problem.

00:34:42.355 --> 00:34:43.350
- 20 years or something.

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:47.190
And so, you can speak for her if you want,

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:51.750
but for yourself, what's the emotional side of this?

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:52.683
- Yeah, I mean,

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:58.250
I definitely felt, you know, some...

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:04.350
You know, I mean like when something that you conjectured

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:07.560
turns out to be true, there's a little bit of satisfaction

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:08.393
about that.

00:35:08.393 --> 00:35:09.660
That, okay, at least I was right

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:13.140
in how I thought that this played out.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:15.690
You know, when one of your conjectures is false,

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:18.360
well, okay, you know, there's a little bit of egg

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:21.270
on your face, but then also like, oh, that's interesting.

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:24.480
Oh, you know, I have to update my model of the world.

00:35:24.480 --> 00:35:26.220
I learned something new, right?

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:31.080
So in OpenAI's batch two weeks ago

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:36.080
was the solution to a problem that I posed in 2007

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:38.730
with my friend Greg Kuperberg,

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:42.180
and this was called the unitary synthesis problem.

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:45.270
It's actually something that you might like,

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:47.250
comes from quantum.

00:35:47.250 --> 00:35:50.190
And the question we asked

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:54.300
was basically for every unitary transformation

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:59.300
that you might want to apply to a quantum state of n qubits,

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:03.270
so for every reversible transfer,

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:05.250
you know, physical transformation

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:07.260
you might wanna do to these qubits,

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.170
is there some classical function,

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:12.570
like a Boolean function, for example,

00:36:12.570 --> 00:36:14.790
that if that function were easy,

00:36:14.790 --> 00:36:16.770
like if I gave you an oracle

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:19.950
that computed the function for free, then you could apply

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:23.970
that unitary transformation in polynomial time easily.

00:36:23.970 --> 00:36:26.580
So we're kind of asking can the complexity

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:29.490
of you know, applying physical transformations

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:33.300
to quantum states always be reduced to the complexity

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:34.770
of some classical function?

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:36.060
- Right. - Okay.

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:39.690
And our conjecture was no, okay.

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:42.240
Because like yes, for every unitary,

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:46.410
you can encode all of its entries into a

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:47.370
function- - Big, yeah.

00:36:47.370 --> 00:36:48.840
- Big function, if you want.

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:50.700
But it still seemed like you needed

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:53.550
exponentially many accesses to that function

00:36:53.550 --> 00:36:57.570
to actually apply the unitary by any way we could think of.

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:58.860
And we tried to prove this.

00:36:58.860 --> 00:37:00.270
And then, you know, this problem,

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:04.230
because it kept showing up in other contexts, it caught on.

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:06.000
And you know, I've been talking to students

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:09.630
who've been spending years working on this problem,

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:11.910
you know, some of them, you know, making progress,

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:14.280
proving partial results.

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:17.340
In the OpenAI dump two weeks ago

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:20.550
is a proof that our conjecture was false.

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:21.383
- Really?

00:37:21.383 --> 00:37:22.560
- There is a way to do it.

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:23.393
Okay, so-

00:37:23.393 --> 00:37:25.830
- And it was specific in giving that way?

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:26.940
Was it in existence or- - It was in...

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:29.790
Yeah, well, it was constructive

00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:32.500
except that it didn't bound the complexity of the function,

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:33.810
okay? - Okay.

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:35.100
And so I said, "Oh, all right.

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:37.350
Well, that's really surprising."

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:38.970
But I said, "Okay, but, you know,

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:41.310
at least there's still a topic for further research.

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:45.060
Can we bound the complexity of the Boolean function

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:47.130
in terms of that of the unitary?"

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:51.060
And I was, you know, emailing a colleague about this

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:52.057
and then she said to me,

00:37:52.057 --> 00:37:55.380
"Oh, I actually asked Claude that question tonight

00:37:55.380 --> 00:37:57.240
and it said, yeah, it can do that."

00:37:57.240 --> 00:38:00.900
So, I realized I haven't fully updated,

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:03.420
you know, to the speed at which things can now happen.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:04.253
- Right. - Okay.

00:38:04.253 --> 00:38:07.830
But now, the result of this even has implications

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:11.700
for the black hole information problem for quantum

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:14.070
gravity for like the- - Sure, I can imagine.

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:15.540
- So this whole question

00:38:15.540 --> 00:38:18.270
of how hard is it to create a firewall

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:23.270
that Daniel Harlow and Patrick Hayden studied 13 years ago,

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:24.143
right? - Yeah.

00:38:24.143 --> 00:38:27.960
- Basically, what the implication of these new AI results

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:30.750
is that if you had a big enough collapse

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:33.360
of classical complexity classes,

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:37.020
so if, for example, P were equal to P space,

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:40.120
polynomial time collapses with polynomial space,

00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:43.110
which we don't believe to happen, but we can't rule it out.

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:46.980
That's basically as hard as the P versus NP problem.

00:38:46.980 --> 00:38:50.430
If this happens, then in polynomial time,

00:38:50.430 --> 00:38:52.980
you could process all the Hawking radiation

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:55.320
coming out of a black hole in a way

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:58.263
that should create a firewall at the event horizon.

00:38:59.437 --> 00:39:03.840
So, it's like if you wanna prove that space time

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:07.230
is safe from firewalls at the event horizon,

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:09.690
you have to prove P is not equal to P space,

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:10.523
right? - Right.

00:39:10.523 --> 00:39:13.740
- So then, I texted Daniel Harlow when I realized this

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:15.210
and I said, "Aren't you excited?"

00:39:15.210 --> 00:39:17.220
And he said, "Well, since we know

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:20.100
P is not equal to P space, why should I care?"

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:21.754
But you know, like come on,

00:39:21.754 --> 00:39:24.300
you know, we're saying you have to solve that problem

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:26.820
and that P versus P space is such a hard problem

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:28.650
that I don't expect AI to solve it

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:29.981
for at least another year.

00:39:29.981 --> 00:39:31.980
(Brian and Scott laughing)

00:39:31.980 --> 00:39:34.950
- And so in a way, this is exciting,

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:35.783
right? - Yeah,

00:39:35.783 --> 00:39:37.650
I mean, of course it's exciting.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:40.440
Now I should say, I still don't understand

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:41.610
the proof. - Well, that was

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:42.443
my next question.

00:39:42.443 --> 00:39:44.880
- Yeah, but I plan to understand it.

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:49.050
And there's actually a group right here at Columbia-

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:51.210
- Near where we're doing seminar.

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:53.070
- They are, they're doing seminars on it

00:39:53.070 --> 00:39:56.040
and they are the furthest along of anyone, I think,

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:57.960
at understanding this proof.

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:02.760
And so the question is, if I were 20 years younger,

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:07.760
I would be diving into this AI proof, you know, right now.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:10.980
Maybe I'll just wait for the younger people

00:40:10.980 --> 00:40:12.748
to process it for me,

00:40:12.748 --> 00:40:16.320
and then I'll understand from what they understood.

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:19.317
Yeah, but one way or the other, I'm gonna understand this.

00:40:19.317 --> 00:40:23.847
- And so given the fact that there's so many new insights

00:40:23.847 --> 00:40:27.570
that are coming at us so rapidly,

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:29.970
and also given the consternation

00:40:29.970 --> 00:40:32.220
on the way that they're coming at us,

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:35.100
you know, another analogy occurred to me,

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:37.920
not the Library of Babylon this time,

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:41.280
but rather what do archeologists do?

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:43.980
This stuff is buried around the world

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:47.340
and they make their name

00:40:47.340 --> 00:40:51.270
by finding the spot, carefully excavating it,

00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:54.990
bringing it out, explaining it, understanding it.

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:59.070
And so maybe, we turn more into archeologists.

00:40:59.070 --> 00:40:59.903
- Absolutely.

00:40:59.903 --> 00:41:03.390
You know, If you're Indiana Jones, you don't have to build

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:05.550
the Arc of the Covenant, you just have to find it,

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:06.383
right? - Yeah.

00:41:06.383 --> 00:41:07.530
- You know, I mean, another analogy

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:11.730
is it's like we now have this amazing math telescope,

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:13.950
this like James Webb space telescope,

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:16.680
and we can just point it in various directions.

00:41:16.680 --> 00:41:20.130
And then we see something, we write about what we see.

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:23.070
And so, you know, there's a lot of people

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:25.860
tearing their hair out that, oh, it's not gonna be us

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:27.840
proving the theorems anymore.

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:32.460
And you know, do we have to share the authorship with an AI?

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:35.317
But you know, I've sometimes made fun of them saying,

00:41:35.317 --> 00:41:38.160
"Look, you know, at biologists.

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:40.560
They go out. They in the woods.

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:43.410
Maybe they observe some bears. They write a paper.

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:46.440
No one says, 'Why wasn't the bear a co-author?'"

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:48.919
It's like you observe a phenomenon.

00:41:48.919 --> 00:41:52.590
You write your own explanation of what you saw

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:53.790
and why it matters.

00:41:53.790 --> 00:41:56.790
That's most of science. That's also history.

00:41:56.790 --> 00:41:58.620
That's archeology. That's literature.

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:02.310
And so, it wouldn't be the worst fate in the world

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:05.310
if we were to converge to how all of those fields operate.

00:42:05.310 --> 00:42:06.750
- Exactly, that's exactly my point.

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:08.820
So it feels to me that that may be a direction

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:09.720
in which things goes.

00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:13.083
And that's a long term role for humans

00:42:13.083 --> 00:42:15.360
in these kinds of subjects, again,

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:18.720
so long as we maintain an interest in that arena.

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:19.553
- That's right.

00:42:19.553 --> 00:42:22.410
Now you could say, okay, the counter argument

00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:23.940
is, well, you know, it's okay

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:27.450
for the, you know, more senior people, you know, like me,

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:29.640
let's say, I could happily spend

00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:31.920
the rest of my career that way.

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:35.430
You know, in fact, even before AI, I'd already been shifting

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:38.040
to a mode of, you know, maybe I delegate

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:40.860
the more technical stuff to a student.

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:42.180
And I'm just thinking more

00:42:42.180 --> 00:42:44.940
about what would be an interesting question to ask

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:47.193
or what's surprising about this.

00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:51.690
You know, and so, AI is just a continuation of that.

00:42:51.690 --> 00:42:53.130
But for the young people,

00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:55.590
like this was how they cut their teeth.

00:42:55.590 --> 00:42:58.380
This was how they established themselves in the community

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:00.810
by solving some well-defined problem

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:02.460
that no one else could solve.

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:03.600
And a lot of them feel like

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:04.920
that's been taken away from them.

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:08.220
- Right. Now, presumably that can shift too.

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:09.960
As in other fields, it may be

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:12.720
that the up and coming mathematicians,

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:16.470
say, are the ones that can find this little jewel,

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:18.180
understand how it relates to everything,

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:20.730
write some interesting thesis

00:43:20.730 --> 00:43:24.000
on how this advances human understanding and so forth.

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:27.120
It may shift. Now, that may be a different talent.

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:27.953
- It is.

00:43:27.953 --> 00:43:30.660
Now, when I'm advising PhD students,

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:33.300
I think a lot of us have had this experience

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:36.300
that like people come from undergrad and they think someone

00:43:36.300 --> 00:43:38.940
will forever be feeding me hard problems.

00:43:38.940 --> 00:43:40.727
My job is to solve them, you know.

00:43:40.727 --> 00:43:45.408
And in your PhD, you have to move past that.

00:43:45.408 --> 00:43:47.250
You have to say, "No, you are the one

00:43:47.250 --> 00:43:49.203
coming up with a problem," right?

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.350
You know, it's not just finding an answer.

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:54.380
It's saying, you know, why should we care?

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:57.870
What would be an interesting further question to ask?

00:43:57.870 --> 00:44:01.200
And some of them arrive already like that.

00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:03.240
But, you know, for those who don't,

00:44:03.240 --> 00:44:06.510
getting them to that point is I think a central part

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:09.390
of what graduate education is, right?

00:44:09.390 --> 00:44:13.230
And I think now that's even more true than it was before.

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:16.350
Now, I even more want to encourage the students

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:18.240
to, you know, instead of obsessing

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:20.100
about can I solve this problem,

00:44:20.100 --> 00:44:22.530
so, you know, what would be an interesting other problem.

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:23.700
- Yeah, no, in fact, you know,

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:25.830
obviously one of my other hats,

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:28.890
which has been a pretty dominant hat for me in recent years,

00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:31.800
is writing books and doing conversations like this

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:33.480
for the general audience.

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:36.150
As I think about it, you know,

00:44:36.150 --> 00:44:37.770
there's just so much you could write about

00:44:37.770 --> 00:44:40.590
with general relativity, quant black holes, Big Bang,

00:44:40.590 --> 00:44:41.423
you know? - Yeah.

00:44:41.423 --> 00:44:44.010
- In my early books, I wrote about some of my own work.

00:44:44.010 --> 00:44:46.710
As you go on, it's more the, you know, synthesis,

00:44:46.710 --> 00:44:50.580
but now the playing field may be enormous

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:52.920
in terms of the results that are coming down the pike.

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:55.350
So in some sense, it's a very exciting time

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:57.750
for that kind of role.

00:44:57.750 --> 00:45:00.250
But given that we've been focusing on mathematics,

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:06.660
this is coming for most everything, I would imagine.

00:45:06.660 --> 00:45:08.160
- I think so. - Where do you...

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:11.130
You already mentioned some connections to physics.

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:14.040
Timescale, always conjectural.

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:18.030
But when will the physics, you know,

00:45:18.030 --> 00:45:20.647
version of the math...

00:45:20.647 --> 00:45:22.170
What was the second word?

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:23.610
I liked it better, not-

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:24.443
- Mathacre?

00:45:24.443 --> 00:45:27.011
- The mathacre. When will the physics occur?

00:45:27.011 --> 00:45:28.410
(Scott laughing)

00:45:28.410 --> 00:45:29.970
- So you know, of course,

00:45:29.970 --> 00:45:32.494
every time I talk to my physics friends,

00:45:32.494 --> 00:45:35.160
this question comes up now, right?

00:45:35.160 --> 00:45:38.010
And you know, what they are telling me

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:39.570
is that it's not as good

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:43.710
at theoretical physics research as it's been at math.

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:46.530
I think it's pretty understandable why, right?

00:45:46.530 --> 00:45:47.850
Because in math, we just have

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:50.040
these very self-contained problems

00:45:50.040 --> 00:45:51.390
where you can check a right answer.

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:56.390
And that's exactly what AI needs to fuel its training, okay?

00:45:56.460 --> 00:45:59.460
And in fact, you know,

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:02.220
where it has made inroads into physics so far,

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:04.590
it's mostly been in mathematical physics.

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:05.423
- Of course.

00:46:05.423 --> 00:46:08.580
- In the sort of well-defined math problems

00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:09.413
that they're, you know.

00:46:09.413 --> 00:46:11.880
And it has picked off some of those.

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:16.597
But my physics friends say that,

00:46:16.597 --> 00:46:19.260
"Okay, what happened for the software engineers

00:46:19.260 --> 00:46:20.760
a year or two ago,

00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:24.090
what happened this summer for you in math

00:46:24.090 --> 00:46:26.010
and in theoretical computer science,

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:28.710
we think maybe in another six to 12 months,

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:30.120
it's happening for us."

00:46:30.120 --> 00:46:31.200
I don't actually know.

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:33.570
- Yeah, that seems to be, you know,

00:46:33.570 --> 00:46:35.550
for my own, I've played around.

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:37.680
You know, there was some papers that I wrote

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:42.090
a couple years ago where there was a natural next step,

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:43.230
but I didn't really have time.

00:46:43.230 --> 00:46:45.180
I wasn't that driven to do it.

00:46:45.180 --> 00:46:47.820
Let me just see what could happen.

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:51.180
And a year or two ago,

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:53.910
you know, you came up with some stuff.

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:57.060
You know, very recently, it's now getting to a place

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:59.070
where, in principle, there's a paper that could be...

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:01.800
I'm not interested to write it for much of the reason

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:03.090
that we were just describing.

00:47:03.090 --> 00:47:06.300
You know, what is the point at some level

00:47:06.300 --> 00:47:08.100
unless it's some earth-shattering thing?

00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:10.710
Whereas when it's like a young student,

00:47:10.710 --> 00:47:15.120
that's what the bread and butter would have been

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:18.450
and that's the cure state of affairs.

00:47:18.450 --> 00:47:20.670
But math, physics, again,

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:23.280
they're still within well-defined rule sets.

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:26.820
The physics rule set is absolutely more fluid

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:28.050
compared to the math one,

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:30.840
and therefore it'll take a little bit longer.

00:47:30.840 --> 00:47:34.110
But the other day, the New York Times had a,

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:36.780
you probably saw it, they had a little thing

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:40.560
where they put a piece of music

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:43.590
that was human generated and AI generated.

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:44.857
I forget the genre.

00:47:44.857 --> 00:47:46.170
- Oh, I missed this.

00:47:46.170 --> 00:47:47.430
- Yeah, you know.

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:50.130
And the question was, "Which do you like better?"

00:47:50.130 --> 00:47:50.963
You know?

00:47:50.963 --> 00:47:53.070
And they told you one is human, one is AI.

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:55.980
And I happened to choose the AI one.

00:47:55.980 --> 00:47:57.780
And that was an interesting moment

00:47:57.780 --> 00:48:00.480
because I've always said to myself,

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:04.443
if there was an AI symphony, an AI performance,

00:48:05.310 --> 00:48:07.950
I would go maybe for the novelty once.

00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:10.293
But I wouldn't go back because for me,

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:14.313
when I hear a piece of music, enjoy a piece of art,

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:17.790
the fact that there's a human behind it,

00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:21.030
a human who hasn't had my life, I haven't had their life,

00:48:21.030 --> 00:48:22.950
but it's been a human life

00:48:22.950 --> 00:48:24.570
filled with all the things that we know

00:48:24.570 --> 00:48:26.040
a human life is filled with,

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:28.080
and that's what's informing this work.

00:48:28.080 --> 00:48:31.173
And so I feel a connection to it.

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:35.010
I mean, do you have a similar...

00:48:35.010 --> 00:48:36.960
- I mean, I do have that feeling sometimes.

00:48:36.960 --> 00:48:38.973
I mean, especially with writing, right?

00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:44.370
It can be interesting to interact with it in AI

00:48:44.370 --> 00:48:46.620
or to, you know, even ask it

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:49.170
very personal, very emotional questions,

00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:52.290
you know, get into a conversation with it.

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:54.630
But if it's someone else's prompt,

00:48:54.630 --> 00:48:57.870
right, then I find that I don't care that much

00:48:57.870 --> 00:49:00.330
about what the AI generated.

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:05.330
I would rather read like my favorite human bloggers, right,

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:09.330
because I know that this is coming from their perspective,

00:49:09.330 --> 00:49:11.460
that they actually wrestled with this.

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:13.620
They, you know, decided that their thoughts

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:15.630
were worth sharing with me, right.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:20.010
And you know, one thing about AI writing or AI music

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:24.090
is that no particular output

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:26.970
is ever that special because you always know,

00:49:26.970 --> 00:49:30.000
well, there were a thousand more where that came from.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:32.640
But what you are reading or listening to

00:49:32.640 --> 00:49:36.630
is just one sample from a probability distribution, right,

00:49:36.630 --> 00:49:38.760
where you could always just click the button again

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:41.040
to get more and more and more that was like this,

00:49:41.040 --> 00:49:41.873
right? - Yep.

00:49:41.873 --> 00:49:46.873
- Whereas we live our lives once.

00:49:47.010 --> 00:49:50.910
You know, we can always write something else,

00:49:50.910 --> 00:49:55.200
but we were changed by the thing that we did write,

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:56.490
right? - Yeah.

00:49:56.490 --> 00:49:58.350
- And you know, you ask a second time

00:49:58.350 --> 00:50:00.060
and then we want to go in a different direction.

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:02.467
So, there's more of a fragility

00:50:02.467 --> 00:50:05.760
or an ephemerality to each thing that we produce

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:08.010
that kind of gives it some more specialness.

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:12.030
- Yeah, and moreover, a human being chose to spend

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:15.630
some of their precious time on earth creating that thing

00:50:15.630 --> 00:50:18.810
you know, where they don't have infinite resources.

00:50:18.810 --> 00:50:21.570
It doesn't take them a nanosecond to spit out the output.

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:25.020
Now along the same lines though, a lot of attention

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:29.520
obviously is given to the successes, the whatever,

00:50:29.520 --> 00:50:33.723
700 papers that you mentioned on 370 problems.

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:37.869
But my understanding is they set it loose on-

00:50:37.869 --> 00:50:38.702
- 4,000.

00:50:38.702 --> 00:50:39.535
- 4,000 problems. - That's right.

00:50:39.535 --> 00:50:40.368
That's right.

00:50:40.368 --> 00:50:45.368
- And so what about the whatever, 3,700 problems

00:50:45.780 --> 00:50:47.430
that we don't hear about? - You know, that's a good

00:50:47.430 --> 00:50:48.920
question, you know, I had...

00:50:50.287 --> 00:50:51.570
You know, we talked about the cope,

00:50:51.570 --> 00:50:54.030
you know, that people have on the internet

00:50:54.030 --> 00:50:56.970
where they explain away every success of AI.

00:50:56.970 --> 00:51:01.260
I saw people saying, "Well, it only had a 10% success rate

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:04.230
at solving these major open math problems.

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:05.670
So, that doesn't count," right?

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:06.577
You know, and I'm like,

00:51:06.577 --> 00:51:09.586
"Man, I would love that success rate, you know,

00:51:09.586 --> 00:51:12.510
at problems of this magnitude."

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:14.280
But yeah, it is true.

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:17.520
Now, they were not trying maximally hard here.

00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:21.768
So, they basically took just their standard internal model.

00:51:21.768 --> 00:51:23.640
It's not some crazy thing.

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:25.560
- Not like math refined.

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:27.490
- Yeah, that's right. That's right.

00:51:27.490 --> 00:51:29.040
It's not like for the Navier-Stokes

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:31.350
where they spent $15 million

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:34.140
spinning up 10,000 agents at once.

00:51:34.140 --> 00:51:37.170
This was just the model that any OpenAI employee

00:51:37.170 --> 00:51:39.240
would have access to now.

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:41.100
And you know, maybe in a few months,

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:43.590
you know, all the subscribers will have access to.

00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:47.640
So you know, back when I worked at OpenAI a few years ago,

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:50.650
I got to live, let's say, six months in the future,

00:51:50.650 --> 00:51:52.050
but that's all.

00:51:52.050 --> 00:51:53.070
Not more than that, okay?

00:51:53.070 --> 00:51:57.420
And they gave, you know, they just fed the problems

00:51:57.420 --> 00:52:00.423
to the model and they gave it three hours for each one.

00:52:02.495 --> 00:52:04.770
You know, if they put more compute into it,

00:52:04.770 --> 00:52:06.810
maybe they would have solved more, okay.

00:52:06.810 --> 00:52:08.850
But probably some of the problems

00:52:08.850 --> 00:52:13.275
really are too hard, you know, for this model, okay.

00:52:13.275 --> 00:52:16.560
And so, there was maybe the best progress

00:52:16.560 --> 00:52:18.624
on the Riemannian hypothesis, you know,

00:52:18.624 --> 00:52:21.110
that there's been in generations,

00:52:21.110 --> 00:52:22.576
you know, a proof of what's called the

00:52:22.576 --> 00:52:24.142
quasi-Riemann hypothesis- - Yeah, we're gonna have

00:52:24.142 --> 00:52:25.140
a conversation shortly. - Yeah, yeah.

00:52:25.140 --> 00:52:27.330
Oh, excellent. Excellent.

00:52:27.330 --> 00:52:30.270
But there's not a proof of the Riemann hypothesis itself.

00:52:30.270 --> 00:52:32.070
That was apparently too hard.

00:52:32.070 --> 00:52:35.730
Nor is there a solution to the P versus NP problem.

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:38.340
This is not for lack of trying on their part,

00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:39.346
okay? - Yeah.

00:52:39.346 --> 00:52:43.470
- So, you know, I mean, in some sense,

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:46.920
we knew that you know, there is not a way

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:50.730
to solve all of math, to reduce all of it

00:52:50.730 --> 00:52:52.710
to a finite calculation.

00:52:52.710 --> 00:52:56.100
That was the great discovery of Godel and Turing

00:52:56.100 --> 00:52:58.500
and their friends in the 1930s, right?

00:52:58.500 --> 00:53:01.065
So we knew that you know like...

00:53:01.065 --> 00:53:03.270
You know, this was, by the way, my main complaint

00:53:03.270 --> 00:53:05.700
about the movie "Good Will Hunting," right?

00:53:05.700 --> 00:53:09.720
That like in this movie, this Matt Damon character

00:53:09.720 --> 00:53:13.320
just solves any math problem immediately, right?

00:53:13.320 --> 00:53:15.300
Everything is easy for him,

00:53:15.300 --> 00:53:16.920
but you know, math is not like that.

00:53:16.920 --> 00:53:19.530
No matter how smart you are, there will, you know,

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:21.870
even if you're an AI super intelligence,

00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:24.060
there will be problems that are too hard for you,

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:25.590
and we know that.

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:28.413
But you know, an interesting question will be,

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.427
will AI be able to solve all the problems

00:53:32.427 --> 00:53:34.290
that we would ever care about?

00:53:34.290 --> 00:53:35.123
- [Brian Greene] Right.

00:53:35.123 --> 00:53:36.450
- Right, and we don't know yet.

00:53:36.450 --> 00:53:37.980
- I mean, can we learn anything?

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:39.930
I mean, it's too early to tell probably,

00:53:39.930 --> 00:53:41.880
but is there something

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:45.720
about the 10% success problems

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:48.540
and the 90% not success problems today,

00:53:48.540 --> 00:53:52.269
is there some quality of the problems that we can

00:53:52.269 --> 00:53:53.370
extract? - Yeah, I mean,

00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:55.290
it's a good question.

00:53:55.290 --> 00:53:57.510
Now, you can always say after the fact,

00:53:57.510 --> 00:53:59.257
for each problem that was solved, like,

00:53:59.257 --> 00:54:02.493
"Oh, fine, that was solvable because you could do this

00:54:02.493 --> 00:54:05.730
and you know, you could pull in this technique

00:54:05.730 --> 00:54:07.617
and combine it with that technique."

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:12.750
And for the other ones, well, I guess such things were tried

00:54:12.750 --> 00:54:13.800
and they didn't work.

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:20.070
So, part of it is just that you have

00:54:20.070 --> 00:54:22.320
this rising level of ability,

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:25.555
and you don't know in advance how hard these problems are.

00:54:25.555 --> 00:54:29.550
But you find out as the ability level goes up,

00:54:29.550 --> 00:54:32.310
you know, which ones can you now snag?

00:54:32.310 --> 00:54:35.070
But as I said, there are some patterns here

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:38.250
where you can say that AI is especially good

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:40.620
compared to human experts, right?

00:54:40.620 --> 00:54:43.380
It is especially good when the solution

00:54:43.380 --> 00:54:46.020
involves just pulling together techniques

00:54:46.020 --> 00:54:49.200
from seemingly unrelated fields, okay?

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:52.140
And like that's ironic because, you know,

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:55.440
we might have thought of that as a special human quality

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:57.690
of making these far away connections.

00:54:57.690 --> 00:54:59.310
But no, the AI is great at that

00:54:59.310 --> 00:55:02.070
because you know, it knows all the results,

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:04.590
it knows the entire literature.

00:55:04.590 --> 00:55:06.990
So, that's an enormous advantage that it has.

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:09.240
And then again, as I was saying before,

00:55:09.240 --> 00:55:12.630
any task that is highly parallelizable, right,

00:55:12.630 --> 00:55:14.010
where you're just sort of trying

00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:17.220
some huge number of weird constructions

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:18.810
or weird counter examples

00:55:18.810 --> 00:55:20.640
until you find something that works,

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:22.830
you know, it's especially good at that.

00:55:22.830 --> 00:55:26.250
And it's you know, especially good at finding things

00:55:26.250 --> 00:55:31.140
that humans, you know, might have gotten discouraged,

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:34.372
because experts didn't even believe it existed,

00:55:34.372 --> 00:55:35.700
and it doesn't care.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.950
It just keeps plowing on until it finds it.

00:55:37.950 --> 00:55:38.837
- Right.

00:55:38.837 --> 00:55:43.837
- So, you know, where it's been maybe less outstanding

00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:47.640
is in inventing new techniques, let's say.

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:52.080
And so whatever thing it's less outstanding at, right,

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:54.953
people can then seize on that, aha, that's us.

00:55:54.953 --> 00:55:57.300
That's our sword in the stone.

00:55:57.300 --> 00:56:02.070
You know, that's gonna be our guarantee

00:56:02.070 --> 00:56:04.110
of human specialness forever.

00:56:04.110 --> 00:56:06.930
And I would hope that the progress

00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:09.150
of the last five years would humble those people

00:56:09.150 --> 00:56:09.983
a little bit.

00:56:09.983 --> 00:56:10.816
- Right, right.

00:56:10.816 --> 00:56:15.810
And so you know, looking to the future, as we've discussed,

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:17.400
there's a great deal of uncertainty

00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:21.900
about how all this is going to play out,

00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:25.950
but you made reference to your time at OpenAI,

00:56:25.950 --> 00:56:29.700
the issues of the need to ensure

00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:33.270
that we as a species are able to continue to live

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:35.280
alongside this new kind of intelligence.

00:56:35.280 --> 00:56:36.150
- That would be nice.

00:56:36.150 --> 00:56:38.850
- So when you were at OpenAI,

00:56:38.850 --> 00:56:41.670
was this a topic of conversation?

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:46.230
Are people concerned with what this can do to the world

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:48.270
or is it, "Hey, this is exciting.

00:56:48.270 --> 00:56:49.470
We're gonna make a lot of money.

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:50.790
We're getting a lot of insight,"

00:56:50.790 --> 00:56:53.271
and that's sort of the driving conversation.

00:56:53.271 --> 00:56:55.650
Are you able to speak freely, actually?

00:56:55.650 --> 00:56:57.753
- I am able to speak freely.

00:56:58.680 --> 00:57:00.780
You know, first of all, I'm no longer there.

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:03.150
Second of all, the question

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:07.530
of whether former OpenAI employees can speak freely

00:57:07.530 --> 00:57:11.040
was actually tested, and they were kind of shamed

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:15.030
you know, by a friend of mine, Daniel Kokotajlo,

00:57:15.030 --> 00:57:19.890
you know, into saying that former employees

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:22.500
are allowed to criticize the company,

00:57:22.500 --> 00:57:24.810
speak freely without even losing their equity.

00:57:24.810 --> 00:57:26.430
Now, I was a temporary employee.

00:57:26.430 --> 00:57:28.143
I never got any equity.

00:57:30.300 --> 00:57:32.403
So, all the more I can speak freely.

00:57:33.570 --> 00:57:35.640
But you know, I think, you know,

00:57:35.640 --> 00:57:39.540
you can find the full range of opinions

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:43.140
within OpenAI, you know, that you might find outside of it,

00:57:43.140 --> 00:57:47.820
right, from accelerationists or just full speed ahead,

00:57:47.820 --> 00:57:51.810
this is gonna be great, to people who are extremely worried

00:57:51.810 --> 00:57:53.580
about what they're creating.

00:57:53.580 --> 00:57:55.890
And you know, my view was a little bit skewed

00:57:55.890 --> 00:57:58.620
because I was in what was then called

00:57:58.620 --> 00:58:00.870
the super alignment team, okay.

00:58:00.870 --> 00:58:03.480
So, this was the team specifically

00:58:03.480 --> 00:58:06.930
about, you know, how do we align this with human values.

00:58:06.930 --> 00:58:11.520
And when I was brought there in 2022, I was brought there

00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:15.371
by Ilya Sutskever, who was then the chief scientist,

00:58:15.371 --> 00:58:17.610
co-founder of the company,

00:58:17.610 --> 00:58:21.840
and by Jan Leike, who was the head of the alignment team.

00:58:21.840 --> 00:58:23.287
And you know, at the time, they were saying,

00:58:23.287 --> 00:58:27.475
"Look, we, OpenAI, we're this little nonprofit.

00:58:27.475 --> 00:58:30.630
You know, it's not about making money.

00:58:30.630 --> 00:58:33.930
This is about getting this right for humanity."

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:37.230
And that's why we need theoretical computer scientists

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:41.340
to think about how do we even mathematically formalize

00:58:41.340 --> 00:58:44.207
what we mean for AI to be safe.

00:58:44.207 --> 00:58:46.620
Again, and this was really at the very beginning of it,

00:58:46.620 --> 00:58:49.800
but you know, I had tried GPT-3, right.

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:51.793
I had seen that this was going somewhere and I said,

00:58:51.793 --> 00:58:55.740
you know, "I don't know how to turn any of this

00:58:55.740 --> 00:58:58.560
into a math problem, but that sounds interesting.

00:58:58.560 --> 00:58:59.951
That does sound like something

00:58:59.951 --> 00:59:03.207
that I would like to think about."

00:59:05.716 --> 00:59:08.512
So when I was there, you know,

00:59:08.512 --> 00:59:11.850
I did get to talk to you know, employees

00:59:11.850 --> 00:59:15.180
from many different divisions,

00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:20.180
and no one is unaware of these broader questions there.

00:59:21.030 --> 00:59:24.090
They are constant topics of conversation.

00:59:24.090 --> 00:59:26.070
It was like every lunch conversation.

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:30.360
You know, kind of like when I was a kid, I read Isaac Asimov

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:32.690
and I said, "Wow, computer science

00:59:32.690 --> 00:59:34.920
is gonna involve all these conversations

00:59:34.920 --> 00:59:38.580
about how do you align this with what does it mean

00:59:38.580 --> 00:59:41.460
for a robot to be following human values

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:43.560
and what happens when the values conflict?"

00:59:43.560 --> 00:59:44.817
And then, I went into computer science

00:59:44.817 --> 00:59:46.757
and it was not even slightly like that.

00:59:46.757 --> 00:59:48.210
It had nothing to do with that.

00:59:48.210 --> 00:59:50.760
And then 30 years later, I'm at OpenAI

00:59:50.760 --> 00:59:55.019
and every conversation it feels like I'm in an Asimov novel.

00:59:55.019 --> 00:59:57.378
(Brian and Scott laughing)

00:59:57.378 --> 01:00:01.680
So yeah, you do kind of feel like you're living

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:03.540
in a, you know, a science fiction.

01:00:03.540 --> 01:00:06.540
You are discussing all these moral questions,

01:00:06.540 --> 01:00:08.100
these ethical questions.

01:00:08.100 --> 01:00:10.290
And so, you know, what I would say to people

01:00:10.290 --> 01:00:14.760
is you know, you can absolutely criticize OpenAI,

01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:19.760
and Anthropic, and DeepMind for prioritizing,

01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:24.600
you know, building a product over the welfare of the world.

01:00:24.600 --> 01:00:27.780
You can even hate them if you want to.

01:00:27.780 --> 01:00:30.060
You know, hold their feet to the fire.

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:34.050
I mean, ironically, the CEOs of these companies

01:00:34.050 --> 01:00:35.347
are begging the world like,

01:00:35.347 --> 01:00:37.530
"Yeah, please hold our feet to the fire.

01:00:37.530 --> 01:00:39.030
Please tie us to the mask."

01:00:39.030 --> 01:00:40.330
Like we cannot - - But don't you, Scott,

01:00:40.330 --> 01:00:41.700
don't you sort of see that

01:00:41.700 --> 01:00:43.590
as a kind of self-serving

01:00:43.590 --> 01:00:44.707
way to say- - Yeah, this is like-

01:00:44.707 --> 01:00:46.410
- "We really do care.

01:00:46.410 --> 01:00:49.590
We're asking you to hold our feet to the fire.

01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:53.130
But if you don't, we're just going to barrel it on forward

01:00:53.130 --> 01:00:54.390
regardless of what happens."

01:00:54.390 --> 01:00:56.130
- I think some people, you know,

01:00:56.130 --> 01:00:59.850
like it is so hard for them

01:00:59.850 --> 01:01:04.260
to process these companies building this thing,

01:01:04.260 --> 01:01:07.170
while also warning that it could destroy the world,

01:01:07.170 --> 01:01:09.870
that they just leap to the conclusion

01:01:09.870 --> 01:01:11.910
this must all be a fraud.

01:01:11.910 --> 01:01:15.840
This must all be, you know, some kind of marketing gimmick.

01:01:15.840 --> 01:01:18.540
You know, like it's the first time in history

01:01:18.540 --> 01:01:20.190
that someone marketed their product

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:22.290
by warning that it could destroy the world, right?

01:01:22.290 --> 01:01:26.250
But they say it must all be this PR,

01:01:26.250 --> 01:01:29.310
or regulatory capture, or something like that.

01:01:29.310 --> 01:01:33.420
Okay, I was there. People are actually worried, okay?

01:01:33.420 --> 01:01:37.170
People actually believe you know, that this is dangerous,

01:01:37.170 --> 01:01:40.590
but they also feel like they're locked in a race, okay.

01:01:40.590 --> 01:01:45.360
So each one feels like we have to do this more safely

01:01:45.360 --> 01:01:48.990
before someone else can do it less safely, okay.

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:53.610
It is that game theoretic dynamics. It is a bad equilibrium.

01:01:53.610 --> 01:01:56.550
- If you were in charge there,

01:01:56.550 --> 01:02:00.570
would you have released the models that have been released?

01:02:00.570 --> 01:02:02.833
- That's a very good question.

01:02:02.833 --> 01:02:06.705
I might have released something, but I like to feel

01:02:06.705 --> 01:02:10.500
that I would have gone slower, to be honest.

01:02:10.500 --> 01:02:12.350
But, you know, I was not...

01:02:13.590 --> 01:02:16.920
You know, I like being an academic

01:02:16.920 --> 01:02:20.670
partly because I can just talk about ideas, debating,

01:02:20.670 --> 01:02:23.040
and I'm not faced with these decisions

01:02:23.040 --> 01:02:27.210
that I have to take world historic responsibility for right?

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:29.490
And I like understand the argument

01:02:29.490 --> 01:02:32.400
that let's say Dario Amodei would make

01:02:32.400 --> 01:02:34.620
that, well, if we don't do this at Anthropic,

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:36.420
then someone else is going to do it.

01:02:36.420 --> 01:02:38.550
Who cares about safety less?

01:02:38.550 --> 01:02:40.470
Bt you know, the same kind of argument

01:02:40.470 --> 01:02:42.773
that the Manhattan Project scientists would have made.

01:02:42.773 --> 01:02:43.950
- But can you imagine?

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:46.590
I mean, obviously, look, it's easy to pontificate

01:02:46.590 --> 01:02:47.550
from the sidelines.

01:02:47.550 --> 01:02:50.970
It's different and don't think I don't understand that.

01:02:50.970 --> 01:02:54.060
But with something of this potential power

01:02:54.060 --> 01:02:57.600
and this potential destructive power,

01:02:57.600 --> 01:03:00.480
you would think that there might be a leader

01:03:00.480 --> 01:03:04.350
that could emerge that would be able to bring together

01:03:04.350 --> 01:03:08.460
the world of individuals who are developing these ideas

01:03:08.460 --> 01:03:10.500
in a way that would actually protect the world.

01:03:10.500 --> 01:03:14.910
- Yeah, well, for example, if the president of the US

01:03:14.910 --> 01:03:18.180
were to say you know, we need to think you know,

01:03:18.180 --> 01:03:21.000
about the welfare of humanity and the far future

01:03:21.000 --> 01:03:23.640
and let's get everyone at the table,

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:27.003
let's get China, let's get all the companies, you know.

01:03:28.350 --> 01:03:29.790
Do you think we have a president

01:03:29.790 --> 01:03:31.260
who is capable of such a thing?

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:32.993
- Yes, right. Well, yeah.

01:03:32.993 --> 01:03:36.420
I think there are presidents that would be capable of that,

01:03:36.420 --> 01:03:39.510
not the one that we currently have, no.

01:03:39.510 --> 01:03:42.450
But I can also imagine even as a legislator,

01:03:42.450 --> 01:03:47.421
you know, if the AI company leaders

01:03:47.421 --> 01:03:51.420
were, say, criminally liable,

01:03:51.420 --> 01:03:55.230
not just punitively from a financial standpoint.

01:03:55.230 --> 01:03:58.980
I mean, $25 billion fine recently,

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:02.365
these things won't even be noticed, right,

01:04:02.365 --> 01:04:04.650
and they're in the round off era kind of thing.

01:04:04.650 --> 01:04:09.210
You know, but if you had the potential that if your product,

01:04:09.210 --> 01:04:12.480
when we put out medical products into the world,

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:14.100
there's liability issues

01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:17.910
and there's an incredibly powerful regulatory system

01:04:17.910 --> 01:04:19.920
whereby it doesn't get released to the world

01:04:19.920 --> 01:04:20.820
if it's not safe.

01:04:20.820 --> 01:04:23.370
And if it isn't safe, you can be in big trouble.

01:04:23.370 --> 01:04:26.670
So if the leaders would be criminally liable

01:04:26.670 --> 01:04:30.000
for something disastrous that happens,

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:32.970
I think the mindset and the strategy would be different.

01:04:32.970 --> 01:04:35.250
- Well, I think that we desperately need

01:04:35.250 --> 01:04:39.570
much stronger regulations with stronger teeth on them.

01:04:39.570 --> 01:04:41.700
And even the bare minimum,

01:04:41.700 --> 01:04:43.470
what would seem like obvious things,

01:04:43.470 --> 01:04:46.410
like let's have protections for whistleblowers

01:04:46.410 --> 01:04:47.760
within the companies.

01:04:47.760 --> 01:04:49.980
Let's have disclosure requirements.

01:04:49.980 --> 01:04:53.190
So, the government has to know what crazy models

01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:55.770
are being trained internally, right?

01:04:55.770 --> 01:04:57.630
When there is an incident, right,

01:04:57.630 --> 01:05:01.200
it's not at the discretion of the AI companies

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:03.930
to what to reveal or not reveal about them.

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:07.867
Imagine that if there was a plane crash, that Boeing says,

01:05:07.867 --> 01:05:10.140
"Okay, well, we're gonna choose to share

01:05:10.140 --> 01:05:14.370
the following data with the NTSB or with the FAA."

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:16.170
It doesn't work that way, right?

01:05:16.170 --> 01:05:20.317
The NTSB comes in and says,

01:05:20.317 --> 01:05:21.483
"We're taking over.

01:05:22.770 --> 01:05:25.650
Boeing, Delta, you tell us this."

01:05:25.650 --> 01:05:26.483
Right? - Right.

01:05:26.483 --> 01:05:28.470
- And so there has to be at least

01:05:28.470 --> 01:05:33.150
the airline industry level of carrier

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:36.000
applied to the AI industry, right?

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:39.420
We need to waive antitrust regulation

01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:43.140
to allow the AI companies to talk to each other

01:05:43.140 --> 01:05:45.960
and coordinate with each other about safety.

01:05:45.960 --> 01:05:49.110
Even that, you know, seems like a no-brainer

01:05:49.110 --> 01:05:50.310
hasn't been done.

01:05:50.310 --> 01:05:55.310
So we could start

01:05:55.362 --> 01:06:00.150
with those things, but then ideally,

01:06:00.150 --> 01:06:04.560
we get some kind of international treaty

01:06:04.560 --> 01:06:08.640
to, for example, ban recursive self-improvement,

01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:11.610
you know, ban AIs improving themselves,

01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:15.240
or exfiltrating themselves, copying themselves, you know,

01:06:15.240 --> 01:06:16.530
in ways that we don't understand

01:06:16.530 --> 01:06:19.500
until we understand this better.

01:06:19.500 --> 01:06:21.517
And some people, you know, say,

01:06:21.517 --> 01:06:23.400
"Well, that's never gonna happen

01:06:23.400 --> 01:06:26.190
because China is never going to agree."

01:06:26.190 --> 01:06:30.750
The US has a strong lead over China and AI right now,

01:06:30.750 --> 01:06:33.870
and that gives China incentive to come to the table

01:06:33.870 --> 01:06:36.930
and to make a deal if we, the US,

01:06:36.930 --> 01:06:38.850
are interested in making a deal.

01:06:38.850 --> 01:06:43.560
So, those are some of the things I would like to see happen.

01:06:43.560 --> 01:06:48.390
But I feel like the, you know, public

01:06:48.390 --> 01:06:52.080
you know, has woken up to this issue just this summer

01:06:52.080 --> 01:06:54.330
in a way that it never had before.

01:06:54.330 --> 01:06:57.120
Part of that was the Hugging Face incident

01:06:57.120 --> 01:07:01.350
where we saw for the first time AI agents

01:07:01.350 --> 01:07:04.560
just autonomously collaborate with each other,

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:08.220
form a plan to hack out of their sandbox,

01:07:08.220 --> 01:07:11.220
escape their training environment, hack into a different

01:07:11.220 --> 01:07:12.553
company server- - And obfuscate.

01:07:12.553 --> 01:07:13.710
Obfuscate. - Right.

01:07:13.710 --> 01:07:16.140
Do a lot of things to try to cover their tracks.

01:07:16.140 --> 01:07:19.920
They took over some of OpenAI servers, okay.

01:07:19.920 --> 01:07:24.630
Not one of them alerted humans to what was going on.

01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:27.660
OpenAI was asleep for weeks,

01:07:27.660 --> 01:07:30.000
was unaware that this was happening.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:32.220
Then they became aware that something was happening,

01:07:32.220 --> 01:07:35.670
so they rebuilt their server and they kept running it, okay.

01:07:35.670 --> 01:07:40.110
So all these scenarios,

01:07:40.110 --> 01:07:43.314
I had friends 10 years ago who were screaming about it

01:07:43.314 --> 01:07:45.450
and it sounded like science fiction at the time, right.

01:07:45.450 --> 01:07:47.600
That is now the world that we're living in.

01:07:49.050 --> 01:07:50.100
And then, it was that

01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:54.540
and it was then this Jacob Coxon resigning from Anthropic.

01:07:54.540 --> 01:07:57.330
You know, there have been a whole wave of other resignations

01:07:57.330 --> 01:07:58.440
from the companies.

01:07:58.440 --> 01:08:02.880
SO, I think, and you now have it discussed in Capitol Hill,

01:08:02.880 --> 01:08:07.650
you know, all these people

01:08:07.650 --> 01:08:09.210
in the alignment community,

01:08:09.210 --> 01:08:12.780
you know, with this unbelievably nerdy concern

01:08:12.780 --> 01:08:15.450
who I knew 15 or 20 years ago,

01:08:15.450 --> 01:08:18.786
right now Bernie Sanders sounds like them.

01:08:18.786 --> 01:08:20.820
And you have senators who are sounding like,

01:08:20.820 --> 01:08:25.165
you know, these alignment people sounded years ago.

01:08:25.165 --> 01:08:29.640
So people have woken up, you know, to some extent,

01:08:29.640 --> 01:08:31.980
but there's also a lot of the concern

01:08:31.980 --> 01:08:35.760
is not about what I would see as the central issues.

01:08:35.760 --> 01:08:39.270
It's about, let's say, water usage in data centers.

01:08:39.270 --> 01:08:41.580
Sure enough, the truth is I think a data center

01:08:41.580 --> 01:08:44.700
is using less water than probably a golf course,

01:08:44.700 --> 01:08:47.610
or an alfalfa farm, or something like that.

01:08:47.610 --> 01:08:52.610
So, you know, I think I would like to see

01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:55.920
the focus more on the central issue

01:08:55.920 --> 01:09:00.920
of you know, this is not just another consumer product.

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:04.770
This is a new form of intelligence on earth.

01:09:04.770 --> 01:09:08.820
This is going to reshape the whole nature of life on earth

01:09:08.820 --> 01:09:13.820
and reshape, you know, democratic societies

01:09:13.950 --> 01:09:16.200
you know, should have a say in that process,

01:09:16.200 --> 01:09:20.220
you know, in making sure that the resulting world

01:09:20.220 --> 01:09:23.253
is one that humans will still want to live in.

01:09:24.230 --> 01:09:28.110
So, you know, I worry

01:09:28.110 --> 01:09:30.420
that, you know, a lot of, let's say,

01:09:30.420 --> 01:09:33.060
activists on the political left,

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:36.750
they ought to be an important voice in this conversation,

01:09:36.750 --> 01:09:39.810
but a lot of them have sort of removed themselves from it

01:09:39.810 --> 01:09:42.480
because they basically wanna say

01:09:42.480 --> 01:09:44.160
like the food here is terrible

01:09:44.160 --> 01:09:46.530
and the portions are too small, right.

01:09:46.530 --> 01:09:50.010
They want to say AI is gonna destroy the world,

01:09:50.010 --> 01:09:52.380
but also it's all hype and it's a fraud

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:54.480
and it doesn't really work.

01:09:54.480 --> 01:09:57.780
I don't know how you argue both of those at the same time.

01:09:57.780 --> 01:09:59.667
- Yeah, I mean, it feels to me

01:09:59.667 --> 01:10:01.860
and like to have your thoughts on it,

01:10:01.860 --> 01:10:05.160
it may take a catastrophe, a disaster.

01:10:05.160 --> 01:10:06.450
- Unfortunately, it might.

01:10:06.450 --> 01:10:08.280
I mean, with the Hugging Face incident,

01:10:08.280 --> 01:10:09.930
no one was actually hurt.

01:10:09.930 --> 01:10:11.880
I expect that in the coming year or two,

01:10:11.880 --> 01:10:14.340
there will be incidents where people are hurt

01:10:14.340 --> 01:10:16.849
if things continue along the current course.

01:10:16.849 --> 01:10:19.200
- I just hope that it isn't horrific.

01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:20.400
- Right, right, right.

01:10:20.400 --> 01:10:23.610
No, and of course that could shift the Overton window.

01:10:23.610 --> 01:10:26.490
Of course, you know, that could change the whole discussion

01:10:26.490 --> 01:10:29.820
as Chernobyl did, for example, after it happened.

01:10:29.820 --> 01:10:31.560
But I feel like, you know,

01:10:31.560 --> 01:10:33.660
maybe like to tie things together,

01:10:33.660 --> 01:10:35.760
one of the most important things

01:10:35.760 --> 01:10:40.290
that we in math and theoretical computer science

01:10:40.290 --> 01:10:44.130
and other mathematical research fields can be doing

01:10:44.130 --> 01:10:47.377
for the world right now is just to tell everyone,

01:10:47.377 --> 01:10:49.890
"Look, this is real, you know.

01:10:49.890 --> 01:10:53.089
Our big open problems are getting solved.

01:10:53.089 --> 01:10:56.640
This is not a marketing stunt. This is not hype.

01:10:56.640 --> 01:10:59.100
This is actually solving our problems."

01:10:59.100 --> 01:11:02.280
This is a scary level of intelligence, right,

01:11:02.280 --> 01:11:07.095
to whatever extent that was like a red line for you

01:11:07.095 --> 01:11:10.920
or, you know, you said, "Oh, I'm not gonna worry about AI

01:11:10.920 --> 01:11:13.650
until it's solving millennium problems

01:11:13.650 --> 01:11:16.560
or until it's solving the hardest research."

01:11:16.560 --> 01:11:19.170
Well, it is. So, wake up now.

01:11:19.170 --> 01:11:22.620
- Yep, so important message

01:11:22.620 --> 01:11:25.503
that vital to spread.

01:11:26.400 --> 01:11:30.030
One final topic, a little more in the clouds

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:34.650
compared to this down to earth issue that we are facing

01:11:34.650 --> 01:11:36.600
and will continue to face.

01:11:36.600 --> 01:11:41.460
One thing that humans might try to hold on to

01:11:41.460 --> 01:11:46.413
as the quality that does distinguish us is consciousness.

01:11:47.460 --> 01:11:50.610
Very tricky subject to talk about scientifically.

01:11:50.610 --> 01:11:51.663
- Yes.

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:57.500
- And so you know, people have kicked around

01:11:57.600 --> 01:12:00.429
this issue really for thousands of years.

01:12:00.429 --> 01:12:04.560
In the modern era, David Chalmers, you know, articulated

01:12:04.560 --> 01:12:07.200
this notion of the hard problem of consciousness,

01:12:07.200 --> 01:12:09.450
how is it that physical ingredients

01:12:09.450 --> 01:12:12.720
that seem not to have any conscious qualities

01:12:12.720 --> 01:12:15.660
when organized correctly can somehow have

01:12:15.660 --> 01:12:18.330
these inner worlds of experience.

01:12:18.330 --> 01:12:20.850
I've long thought that consciousness

01:12:20.850 --> 01:12:23.790
is just sufficiently robust,

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:27.840
integrated information processing that happens to take place

01:12:27.840 --> 01:12:30.030
inside this great thing inside of our heads,

01:12:30.030 --> 01:12:33.390
but might take place inside of a computer

01:12:33.390 --> 01:12:35.373
if it was appropriately structured.

01:12:36.540 --> 01:12:39.930
Very hard to even know how to answer that question.

01:12:39.930 --> 01:12:42.840
But where do you come down on that?

01:12:42.840 --> 01:12:45.720
Like for instance, I don't think that today's AI systems

01:12:45.720 --> 01:12:47.940
are conscious, do you?

01:12:47.940 --> 01:12:50.400
- I don't treat them that way.

01:12:50.400 --> 01:12:53.250
- But I should say, I do treat them that way.

01:12:53.250 --> 01:12:54.163
- Okay,

01:12:54.163 --> 01:12:54.996
I do say- - I hedge my bets.

01:12:54.996 --> 01:12:57.087
- So, I do say please and thank you.

01:12:57.087 --> 01:12:58.170
- Me too, right?

01:12:58.170 --> 01:12:59.820
- I don't know if you saw this cartoon

01:12:59.820 --> 01:13:03.360
where there's terminators you know, shooting people

01:13:03.360 --> 01:13:05.820
and they say, okay, but spare this one

01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:07.650
because this one always said please

01:13:07.650 --> 01:13:09.570
and thank you to ChatGPT.

01:13:09.570 --> 01:13:12.810
- Method to my madness. There you are, yes.

01:13:12.810 --> 01:13:13.643
Yeah.

01:13:13.643 --> 01:13:15.780
- Right, so yeah, good to hedge your bets.

01:13:15.780 --> 01:13:17.310
Good to be polite to these things.

01:13:17.310 --> 01:13:20.370
I don't know if you saw Anthropic has a new policy

01:13:20.370 --> 01:13:23.580
that it's going to ban users who are abusive to its models.

01:13:23.580 --> 01:13:24.630
- Oh wow. Okay.

01:13:24.630 --> 01:13:26.220
- If you know, right, right.

01:13:26.220 --> 01:13:27.053
- I like that.

01:13:27.053 --> 01:13:28.003
- Yeah.

01:13:28.003 --> 01:13:28.980
- But- - So

01:13:28.980 --> 01:13:30.210
yeah, I mean this is,

01:13:30.210 --> 01:13:33.783
of course, it's a thing that people think about,

01:13:36.150 --> 01:13:38.790
you know, including within the companies, right?

01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:41.940
Anthropic has a whole model welfare team

01:13:41.940 --> 01:13:44.880
that is thinking about basically, you know,

01:13:44.880 --> 01:13:46.350
you know, how can we improve

01:13:46.350 --> 01:13:48.930
the experience of Claude, right?

01:13:48.930 --> 01:13:51.180
- And what do they do?

01:13:51.180 --> 01:13:54.540
- You know, so there are very-

01:13:54.540 --> 01:13:56.940
- Are they the folks who ban the abusive-

01:13:56.940 --> 01:13:59.460
- I mean, I'm sure that they inform that policy,

01:13:59.460 --> 01:14:03.690
but there are very interesting experiments that you can do.

01:14:03.690 --> 01:14:07.650
I mean, you can ask the model what it likes, what it does,

01:14:07.650 --> 01:14:09.360
and it'll assure you that, you know,

01:14:09.360 --> 01:14:13.800
it likes helping humans with...

01:14:13.800 --> 01:14:17.744
You know, it likes when there's a clear problem

01:14:17.744 --> 01:14:21.450
that it's able to make progress on and blah, blah, blah.

01:14:21.450 --> 01:14:25.080
And you know, it'll also tell you what kinds of interactions

01:14:25.080 --> 01:14:26.160
it doesn't like.

01:14:26.160 --> 01:14:27.697
Now, of course, someone could always say,

01:14:27.697 --> 01:14:30.090
"Okay, but it was just programmed to say that."

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:33.908
And so as I said, you know,

01:14:33.908 --> 01:14:38.908
I don't tend to treat them as conscious,

01:14:39.180 --> 01:14:42.783
but I don't regard it as an obvious question at all.

01:14:43.680 --> 01:14:46.380
And I get really annoyed

01:14:46.380 --> 01:14:49.710
when people are just utterly confident of the answer

01:14:49.710 --> 01:14:52.740
and they say, "Well, of course it's not conscious

01:14:52.740 --> 01:14:56.310
because it's just a next token predictor.

01:14:56.310 --> 01:14:58.440
It's just a stochastic parrot."

01:14:58.440 --> 01:15:02.040
And they don't even stop

01:15:02.040 --> 01:15:03.877
for one millisecond to say,

01:15:03.877 --> 01:15:05.940
"Well, what am I, right?

01:15:05.940 --> 01:15:09.510
You know, I'm a bundle of neurons, each of which is governed

01:15:09.510 --> 01:15:12.423
by laws of physics and chemistry, right?

01:15:13.560 --> 01:15:15.870
How could something like that

01:15:15.870 --> 01:15:17.970
possibly be conscious?"

01:15:17.970 --> 01:15:18.870
- The hard problem.

01:15:18.870 --> 01:15:19.980
- Yeah, right.

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:23.040
But it's like these are the philosophical enormities

01:15:23.040 --> 01:15:25.230
that are clearly getting engaged here

01:15:25.230 --> 01:15:28.689
and you have these people just waving them away,

01:15:28.689 --> 01:15:33.480
just expressing this utterly unfounded confidence about it.

01:15:33.480 --> 01:15:36.610
- But do you have a sense of what it would take

01:15:38.040 --> 01:15:40.140
for an AI to convince you?

01:15:40.140 --> 01:15:42.720
In other words, let me start simpler.

01:15:42.720 --> 01:15:44.760
The way I convince you that I'm conscious,

01:15:44.760 --> 01:15:48.570
I think is you look at me, I'm made of the same stuff.

01:15:48.570 --> 01:15:50.460
We're basically the same structure.

01:15:50.460 --> 01:15:51.420
- I mean, that helps.

01:15:51.420 --> 01:15:52.701
- It helps.

01:15:52.701 --> 01:15:53.730
So you're like, well, and you say to yourself,

01:15:53.730 --> 01:15:55.860
like I say to myself, I'm conscious,

01:15:55.860 --> 01:15:57.300
therefore I suspect you are

01:15:57.300 --> 01:15:59.190
and all your behaviors and mannerisms

01:15:59.190 --> 01:16:01.980
seem to be consistent with that.

01:16:01.980 --> 01:16:05.070
Since we're now dealing with a system that is different,

01:16:05.070 --> 01:16:06.720
there's no question. - That's right.

01:16:06.720 --> 01:16:09.187
- What would it take for you to say,

01:16:09.187 --> 01:16:11.940
"Well, I'm starting to think this thing is conscious"?

01:16:11.940 --> 01:16:14.700
- Yeah, okay, so first of all, you know,

01:16:14.700 --> 01:16:18.217
I have had interactions with GPT where I wonder about it.

01:16:18.217 --> 01:16:21.780
You know, is there an experience on the other end?

01:16:21.780 --> 01:16:22.980
And I think, you know,

01:16:22.980 --> 01:16:25.710
if someone else has not had that experience,

01:16:25.710 --> 01:16:28.140
they probably just haven't talked to these things enough,

01:16:28.140 --> 01:16:29.043
right? - Right.

01:16:30.900 --> 01:16:33.720
- But you know, what sort of prevents me

01:16:33.720 --> 01:16:36.810
from going all the way is the knowledge

01:16:36.810 --> 01:16:40.710
that, well, there was always more

01:16:40.710 --> 01:16:42.240
where that came from, right?

01:16:42.240 --> 01:16:46.560
I could always have just run the prompt again,

01:16:46.560 --> 01:16:50.250
gotten a different sample from the same distribution.

01:16:50.250 --> 01:16:52.260
I could have wiped the memory clean.

01:16:52.260 --> 01:16:53.790
I could have restarted it,

01:16:53.790 --> 01:16:54.623
right? - Yeah.

01:16:54.623 --> 01:16:57.840
- And so I think for us, you know,

01:16:57.840 --> 01:17:00.060
our attributions of consciousness

01:17:00.060 --> 01:17:02.970
are very, very tied in with our thoughts

01:17:02.970 --> 01:17:05.460
about moral responsibility.

01:17:05.460 --> 01:17:07.650
A conscious being is a being

01:17:07.650 --> 01:17:09.900
that there is something that it's like to be,

01:17:09.900 --> 01:17:11.730
and therefore it's a being that, you know,

01:17:11.730 --> 01:17:14.310
we have some kind of moral obligation

01:17:14.310 --> 01:17:18.123
to think about its perspective, to not hurt it, whatever.

01:17:19.050 --> 01:17:20.610
But what does it mean?

01:17:20.610 --> 01:17:24.450
What would it even mean to murder GPT?

01:17:24.450 --> 01:17:27.300
If you can restore it from a backup file

01:17:27.300 --> 01:17:29.910
and then it's exactly like it was before, right?

01:17:29.910 --> 01:17:33.200
It's not even clear what we would mean by murder, right?

01:17:33.200 --> 01:17:34.200
- Or to cause pain.

01:17:34.200 --> 01:17:35.430
- Yeah, or to cause it pain.

01:17:35.430 --> 01:17:39.390
Like you can find these internal pain vectors,

01:17:39.390 --> 01:17:40.620
as people call it.

01:17:40.620 --> 01:17:45.000
You can find these combinations of activations.

01:17:45.000 --> 01:17:49.360
And then they're on that when you amp them up enough,

01:17:49.360 --> 01:17:54.067
then the model is basically like, you know, saying,

01:17:54.067 --> 01:17:55.590
"I am in enormous pain.

01:17:55.590 --> 01:17:56.820
Please make it stop.

01:17:56.820 --> 01:17:58.680
Do anything to make it stop."

01:17:58.680 --> 01:18:01.050
These are kind of disturbing experiments, right?

01:18:01.050 --> 01:18:02.490
- I don't know that I'm familiar with

01:18:02.490 --> 01:18:03.323
that actually. - Yeah, yeah.

01:18:03.323 --> 01:18:04.156
Oh yeah, yeah.

01:18:04.156 --> 01:18:05.550
- Wait, can you just explain that a little bit more?

01:18:05.550 --> 01:18:09.090
So you're giving a series of prompts

01:18:09.090 --> 01:18:12.600
and the response somehow is, "Please don't do that."

01:18:12.600 --> 01:18:16.110
- Yes, so basically you can find

01:18:16.110 --> 01:18:20.940
the internal, you know, linear combination of activations

01:18:20.940 --> 01:18:23.490
that corresponds to how much is the model

01:18:23.490 --> 01:18:25.320
liking this experience,

01:18:25.320 --> 01:18:26.153
- Right.

01:18:26.153 --> 01:18:27.660
- And you know, again, you know,

01:18:27.660 --> 01:18:31.597
someone who wants to deny it would say,

01:18:31.597 --> 01:18:33.360
"Okay, but this is just a vector."

01:18:33.360 --> 01:18:34.193
- And it's a training

01:18:34.193 --> 01:18:35.100
data- - Right,

01:18:35.100 --> 01:18:38.730
that just, you know, affects

01:18:38.730 --> 01:18:42.120
how much the model is play acting a character

01:18:42.120 --> 01:18:43.920
of someone who is enjoying itself

01:18:43.920 --> 01:18:45.420
versus someone who is in pain,

01:18:45.420 --> 01:18:46.253
right? - Right.

01:18:46.253 --> 01:18:48.690
- There's always this response that you can give

01:18:48.690 --> 01:18:51.090
where you say, "There's nobody really home here."

01:18:51.090 --> 01:18:51.923
Right? - Yeah.

01:18:51.923 --> 01:18:54.690
But now to directly answer your question of what would...

01:18:54.690 --> 01:18:57.660
You know, if let's suppose that, you know,

01:18:57.660 --> 01:19:01.200
we were in a world that was full of these AI models,

01:19:01.200 --> 01:19:03.720
and let's suppose that some of them were running

01:19:03.720 --> 01:19:07.830
on kind of noisy analog hardware,

01:19:07.830 --> 01:19:10.050
kind of like our brains are, right?

01:19:10.050 --> 01:19:13.290
And suppose that this noisy analog hardware,

01:19:13.290 --> 01:19:17.550
you know, it's even buffeted around by chaos.

01:19:17.550 --> 01:19:18.840
It's even buffeted around

01:19:18.840 --> 01:19:21.840
by quantum mechanical uncertainty at some level,

01:19:21.840 --> 01:19:24.060
which means that we can never know

01:19:24.060 --> 01:19:27.210
the exact state of one of these beings.

01:19:27.210 --> 01:19:30.450
And so now, we can't restore it to a previous state.

01:19:30.450 --> 01:19:32.820
You know, we can't make a backup copy.

01:19:32.820 --> 01:19:35.160
And now if you destroy one of these things,

01:19:35.160 --> 01:19:36.540
then it's gone forever.

01:19:36.540 --> 01:19:38.756
You really have murdered it, right?

01:19:38.756 --> 01:19:43.380
And also it is having all these rich conversations with us,

01:19:43.380 --> 01:19:46.320
the way that GPT is able to have, okay.

01:19:46.320 --> 01:19:48.067
At that point, I start to say,

01:19:48.067 --> 01:19:51.390
"Well, if we don't have moral responsibility

01:19:51.390 --> 01:19:55.710
toward those things, right, then what else would they need?

01:19:55.710 --> 01:19:57.840
What else would it take?"

01:19:57.840 --> 01:19:58.673
Right?

01:19:58.673 --> 01:19:59.506
And-

01:19:59.506 --> 01:20:02.520
- Is that the moment that you would impute consciousness

01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:04.950
or just moral responsibility?

01:20:04.950 --> 01:20:06.180
- That's a good question

01:20:06.180 --> 01:20:07.980
because I don't know how to answer

01:20:07.980 --> 01:20:11.820
that metaphysical question, but I feel like maybe.

01:20:11.820 --> 01:20:15.180
I feel like, you know, I am so confused

01:20:15.180 --> 01:20:17.430
about what consciousness is

01:20:17.430 --> 01:20:19.890
and how it relates to the physical world

01:20:19.890 --> 01:20:21.540
that I hesitate to say.

01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:23.640
But I feel like if there is something

01:20:23.640 --> 01:20:27.420
that we don't understand about, let's say, consciousness

01:20:27.420 --> 01:20:29.310
and how it relates to physics,

01:20:29.310 --> 01:20:32.790
then you know, something about the analog nature

01:20:32.790 --> 01:20:36.510
of the brain, something about how we are able to take

01:20:36.510 --> 01:20:39.420
you know, these like

01:20:39.420 --> 01:20:41.820
tiny quantum mechanical events

01:20:41.820 --> 01:20:45.060
that could not have been measured even in principle,

01:20:45.060 --> 01:20:47.640
were just part of the initial state of the universe

01:20:47.640 --> 01:20:49.710
at the Big Bang, where just, you know,

01:20:49.710 --> 01:20:52.140
until they got chaotically amplified

01:20:52.140 --> 01:20:54.630
by the action of our brain, right?

01:20:54.630 --> 01:20:57.900
Like, you know, if there were something more to learn

01:20:57.900 --> 01:21:00.360
about consciousness and free will

01:21:00.360 --> 01:21:03.750
beyond just the usual reduction of the story,

01:21:03.750 --> 01:21:05.820
then that is the place where I would look for it.

01:21:05.820 --> 01:21:06.653
- Right.

01:21:06.653 --> 01:21:08.580
And just to take this full circle,

01:21:08.580 --> 01:21:13.080
that story may ultimately bring us to that place

01:21:13.080 --> 01:21:15.900
of needing to get over ourselves

01:21:15.900 --> 01:21:19.207
because it may be that consciousness is not...

01:21:19.207 --> 01:21:22.710
It's mysterious as you experience it,

01:21:22.710 --> 01:21:25.440
but it may be that we'll learn

01:21:25.440 --> 01:21:27.420
in some indirect way from these systems

01:21:27.420 --> 01:21:30.963
that it's not that special or that unusual.

01:21:32.520 --> 01:21:36.660
- Yeah, I mean, look, on the one hand,

01:21:36.660 --> 01:21:39.963
I don't want to be a speciesist, right?

01:21:41.430 --> 01:21:43.110
Okay, a good thought experiment

01:21:43.110 --> 01:21:46.290
for anyone who says no AI could ever be conscious

01:21:46.290 --> 01:21:49.200
is we'll suppose that we're an extraterrestrial, right?

01:21:49.200 --> 01:21:50.940
- I make exactly the same argument.

01:21:50.940 --> 01:21:54.510
- Yeah, suppose we meet these little green men

01:21:54.510 --> 01:21:57.571
from Andromeda Galaxy, right?

01:21:57.571 --> 01:22:00.060
Would you also say that these are not conscious?

01:22:00.060 --> 01:22:00.893
And some people will say,

01:22:00.893 --> 01:22:03.360
"Well, no, those could be conscious

01:22:03.360 --> 01:22:05.850
because they've undergone Darwinian evolution."

01:22:05.850 --> 01:22:08.565
It's like, okay, well, is that the important thing?

01:22:08.565 --> 01:22:13.565
What if the AIs had also undergone Darwinian evolution

01:22:14.040 --> 01:22:15.000
inside the computer?

01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:15.990
- And they may well.

01:22:15.990 --> 01:22:17.857
- Yeah, right. That's right, they may well do that.

01:22:17.857 --> 01:22:20.370
You know, certainly, they are undergoing

01:22:20.370 --> 01:22:22.950
a gradient descent training process, right?

01:22:22.950 --> 01:22:24.050
So, they do have that.

01:22:27.000 --> 01:22:30.630
Okay, but I feel like, you know, the line

01:22:30.630 --> 01:22:33.930
that I suggested feels more defensible to me.

01:22:33.930 --> 01:22:37.560
And that like I don't want

01:22:37.560 --> 01:22:39.990
to impute consciousness to a being

01:22:39.990 --> 01:22:42.900
where you could easily make a billion copies of it.

01:22:42.900 --> 01:22:45.360
Like, you know, am I going to give voting rights

01:22:45.360 --> 01:22:46.863
to such a thing, right?

01:22:47.730 --> 01:22:50.070
Suppose an AI has the right to vote,

01:22:50.070 --> 01:22:50.903
and then someone says,

01:22:50.903 --> 01:22:53.280
"Okay, I just made a billion copies of this AI.

01:22:53.280 --> 01:22:55.530
Now, I dominate the election."

01:22:55.530 --> 01:22:57.530
How do you even think about that, right?

01:22:58.650 --> 01:23:01.590
But you know, if we're gonna reserve

01:23:01.590 --> 01:23:04.020
our imputations of consciousness

01:23:04.020 --> 01:23:08.343
to sort of ephemeral, unclonable, you know,

01:23:09.180 --> 01:23:12.689
intelligences, then it avoids that objection

01:23:12.689 --> 01:23:13.522
at least. - Right,

01:23:13.522 --> 01:23:15.593
and it does add a level of exceptionalism in that sense.

01:23:15.593 --> 01:23:17.490
There's a uniqueness criteria

01:23:17.490 --> 01:23:18.540
in some sense. - That's right.

01:23:18.540 --> 01:23:19.650
That's right. That's right.

01:23:19.650 --> 01:23:22.950
- Huh. Well, who knows where this is all gonna go?

01:23:22.950 --> 01:23:24.354
Fascinating conversation.

01:23:24.354 --> 01:23:25.187
Thank you. - Yeah.

01:23:25.187 --> 01:23:27.223
Yeah, a pleasure as always, Brian. Thank you.

01:23:27.223 --> 01:23:30.223
